(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F1: Darlington BWRX-300. OPG's full-year 2025 MD&A (12 Mar 2026): Unit 1 C$6.1B plus C$1.6B shared infrastructure = C$7.7B board-approved release-quality budget, inclusive of interest, escalation and contingency (a nominal as-spent figure, not overnight, not \"2024 dollars\"); four units ~C$20.9B, still being refined; ~1,200 MW. Footnote typo \"$6.1 million\" confirmed (means billion). OUR arithmetic: C$7.7B / 300 MW = ~C$25,700/kW; C$20.9B / 1,200 MW = ~C$17,400/kW (gross, approximate capacity). All Canadian dollars.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U73: C$7.7B unchanged in the March 2026 full-year results; grid connection by end-2030. Added: life-to-date DNNP spend C$1,885M of C$7,700M at 31 Dec 2025. OPG's Q2 2026 MD&A could not be opened (bot check); see A3. \"On budget\" rests on OPG's own word only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U73 extension: OPG's Q2 2026 results release (Aug 2026, newswire copy) does not restate or revise the SMR budget; it reports a C$700M Williams Treaties First Nations investment funding Unit 1 construction. Its \"within the approved budget\" line is about Napanee BESS Phase 1, NOT the SMR. Latest primary budget statement found = March 2026.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U31: Dec 2025 amendment of O. Reg. 53/05 makes DNNP LP OEB rate-regulated and lets regulated prices recover interest on DNNP capex before in-service (from 1 Jan 2026); up to C$3B equity from Building Ontario Fund + Canada Growth Fund; C$5B provincial equity injection to OPG (C$1B paid Dec 2025). OPG's Dec 2025 rate application covers DNNP in-service additions 2027-2031. Its status through 2026 was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U74: the federal Major Projects Office page cites C$7.7B (first SMR incl. common infrastructure) and C$20.9B (four units). The \"three more between 2033 and 2035\" part was NOT found on the page as read. The Ontario government page was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U63: World Nuclear News (secondary, relaying OPG): contingency is inside the budget but unpublished; the budget covers licensing through interest; OPG's estimates fall unit by unit to C$4.1B for Unit 4 (about a third cheaper than Unit 1; OPG's projection, not an outturn). No independent audit is mentioned.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U86: Darlington REFURBISHMENT (a different project). Dossier said final cost forecast $12.7B, ~$100M under $12.8B. WRONG/STALE: OPG's March 2026 MD&A says ~C$150M below the C$12.8B budget, Unit 4 back ahead of schedule in March 2026. Error source: engine read an earlier OEB rate filing. Still a forecast, and still not evidence that a new-build SMR can hold a price.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U107: OEB EB-2020-0290 (Nov 2021): SMR planning costs appropriate to be booked in the Nuclear Development Variance Account; no extra SMR reporting. The backgrounder is SILENT on recovery (the \"did not approve recovering them\" wording is inference from how variance accounts work, not the OEB's words). The IESO/CNSC line is OPG's statement the OEB accepted.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F3: CFPP Class 3 estimate (UAMPS talking points, 2 Jan 2023): $9.3B gross incl. financing, $4.2B DOE cost share + projected IRA benefits, $5.1B net; the economic test result exceeded the $58/MWh (2020$) target; the amended agreement set a NEW test target of $89/MWh (2022$). NOTE: $89 is a reimbursement/withdrawal threshold, not the estimated LCOE itself; the memo does not print the Class 3 LCOE. The ~$30/MWh IRA component has no primary here. Causes per UAMPS: input PPIs (carbon-steel piping +106%) and ~200 bp higher rates. The word \"matured\" is UAMPS's own (\"The CFPP has matured\"), so the scriptTrap calling it IEEFA's inference is wrong on that point. OUR arithmetic: $9.3B / 462 MW = ~$20,100/kW gross incl. financing, nominal.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F3 (2021 step): the $55 to $58/MWh target change came with shrinking from 8 to 6 modules (462 MWe gross), because costs did not fall in proportion to output: a loss of scale inside a modular plant. The 2021 notice gives no dollar year for $55/$58 (the \"2020$\" label is from the 2023 memo).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F4: the Nov 2023 joint UAMPS/NuScale release gives lack of subscription as the reason and does not mention cost (whole first paragraph read). \"Price killed CFPP\" is inference.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F4/U60: Salt Lake Tribune (secondary): 26% subscribed against 80% needed; cities spent hundreds of thousands each since 2015 and could quit before issuing bonds; \"more than $9 billion\" incl. a $4.2B federal commitment. The 80%/370 MW threshold is also in UAMPS's own memo (A9 file).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U105: IEEFA (advocacy) says NuScale cost rose from $9,964 to $21,561/kW 2015-2023 and sets NuScale's 2016 Senate testimony (\"overnight price of approximately $3 billion\", 12 modules, 600 MWe, COD 2024) against $9.3B at cancellation. TRAP: IEEFA's own juxtaposition compares an overnight price with a financing-inclusive total.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F5: MIT-led Joule 2020 (Eash-Gates et al.), abstract on MIT DSpace: nth-of-a-kind US plants cost more, not less, than first-of-a-kind; soft factors over half the 1976-87 rise; containment cost more than doubled 1976-2017. Abstract only (full paper not opened). Large site-built plants, not factory SMRs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U44: Rubin et al. 2015 (Energy Policy 86): nuclear row \"Negative to 6%\" (4 studies, 1972-1996); solar PV median 23% (10-47%, 13 studies); the about -38% is Rubin's formula applied to Grubler's US data, and Grubler says the metaphor is not applicable; mixed nuclear results question SMR learning claims. Note: Rubin's 23% is for solar PV generally; the \"about 20% for modules\" figure comes from Our World in Data (not opened).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a15"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U75: Ziegler & Trancik 2021 (abstract on MIT DSpace): Li-ion cell real price per kWh down ~97% since 1991; 20% per doubling of market size (all cells), 24% (cylindrical), 1992-2016. The OWID solar 20.2%/19.3% figures were not opened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a16"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U70: Lovering, Yip and Nordhaus (Energy Policy 2016, 349 reactors, 7 countries): no inherent escalation trend; South Korea alone shows continuous decline (single utility). Read at Breakthrough's own page (the authors' institute, advocacy-adjacent), not the journal. The WNA Korea $2,157 vs US $7,821/kWe figures were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a17"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U70 (rebuttal): Koomey, Hultman and Grubler, reply in Energy Policy 102 (2017) 640-643, read at the Stanford-hosted manuscript: cherry-picked data, misleading early demonstration reactors, overnight costs incomplete; \"look elsewhere\". Whether Lovering et al. published a rejoinder was NOT checked; present the exchange as a live dispute, not a knockout.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a18"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F6: INL report INL/RPT-25-84701 (June 2025) calls Vogtle milestone data inconclusive. CORRECTION: it relays DOE's Liftoff as Unit 4 \"~20% cheaper\", not \"15-20%\" (error: engine finding). Testing milestones ~38-76% faster. ~$14B to ~$35B, ~150% over, ~$11,000/kW, all \"Mixed USD\"; Sanmen 1&2 ~$2,440/kW in 2018 USD (not like-for-like). Moderate scenario $10,000/$7,800/$6,200 per kWe for 2nd-4th two-unit AP1000 plants, called academic. Lazard separately relays \"~30%\" savings from industry experts (A22).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a19"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U76: DOE Liftoff slides hosted by NARUC, watermarked draft: large reactors cheaper per kW, SMRs smaller overall project cost; next AP1000 \"<$100/MWh with IRA benefits and closer to ~$60/MWh with cost reductions\"; Vogtle ~$11,000/kW and ~$15,000 in 2024 $/kW; next AP1000s ~$8,300/kW; California 2045 modelled $129-150 vs $80-94/MWh. Draft, subsidised, scenario-dependent.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a20"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F7/U38: Lazard 2026 LCOE+ (v19.0): unsubsidised US nuclear $175-255/MWh (Vogtle; high end Unit 3, low end Unit 4), gas CC $51-129, gas peaking $144-276, coal $72-177. The SMR point is a single marker printed \"$2148\" in the extracted text = $214 with footnote 8 (Clinch River, Darlington, Natrium; highly illustrative, not comparable, COD 2030+); PTC sensitivity marker \"$1805\" = $180 with footnote 5 (SMR midpoint under PTC). The month of publication is not in the extracted text.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a21"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F7: Lazard's assumptions and firming analysis: gas $3.45/MMBtu, WACC 7.7%, CCGT high case on $2,400-2,600/kW turbine quotes; renewables plus firming broadly competitive with CCGT, with NO firming charge on CCGT; not a 24/7 single-site firm resource and not a total system-cost analysis.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a22"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F8: OECD NEA Costs of Generating Electricity 2025 (21 countries, real 5% discount rate, plant-level only, transmission excluded). Table ES.2 medians: SMR near-term $13,021/kWe, $146/MWh (11 countries); SMR NOAK $7,092, $84 (9 countries); large near-term $11,188, $141; large NOAK $5,502, $81; CCGT $98; CCGT+CCUS $116; solar PV + battery $94; onshore wind $59; LTO $44. Chapter text gives SMR near-term $142 and large $140 (name which). That NOAK SMRs stay above NOAK large reactors per kW is OUR reading of the table ($7,092 vs $5,502). Dollar year not found in the sections read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a23"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U77: INL/RPT-24-77048 Revision 2 meta-analysis (2022 USD): 300 MWe SMR first-build (\"BOAK\") OCC $5,500/$8,000/$10,000 per kWe, 2050 $2,000/$4,000/$6,250; 1,000 MWe large $5,250/$5,750/$7,750, 2050 $2,250/$3,750/$6,000; learning rate 9.5% SMR (literature spread 3-16%), 8% large. The 9.5% is an assumed average of literature estimates, not an observation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a24"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U65: Nature Energy 2022 stylised least-cost study (42 regions), abstract as posted by Carnegie Science (institutional page, not the journal): wind and solar cheaper at moderate decarbonisation against nuclear at $6,317 and $4,000/kWe; nuclear competitive only in deep decarbonisation (beyond ~80%) without cheap flexibility.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a25"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U102: Goeke, Wimmers, von Hirschhausen, arXiv 2302.14515 (abstract): even at $4,000/kW (2018 USD) and under ten-year builds, the cost-efficient nuclear share in Europe is ~10%; nuclear must run inflexibly near 90% capacity factor. A PREPRINT as read; peer-review status not checked. Fully decarbonised European system, not the US.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a26"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F9/U43: the ~$5.4B Clinch River figure appears on the ConstructConnect page itself (trade press) as \"TVA previously estimated\", before interest and tax credits. A search summary of a Knoxville News Sentinel report attributes both $5.4B and $17,949/kW to TVA's draft 2025 IRP; that article and the IRP were NOT opened. OUR arithmetic: $17,949/kW x 300 MW = ~$5.38B, so the two \"confirming\" figures are one number. ⏭ Superseded by T1-1 and T1-11: the $17,949/kW is printed in TVA's own draft 2025 IRP (a generic first-of-a-kind option, 2024 dollars, overnight); the $5.4B is press arithmetic on it, never TVA's figure.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a27"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G13 / TVA's own position (FY2025 10-K, filed 13 Nov 2025): no Clinch River capital cost, $/kW or overrun-risk statement. What TVA does publish: Board approved up to $350M for the New Nuclear Program ($251M spent); $342M spent on SMR work to 30 Sep 2025, $29M reimbursed by DOE; any construction decision needs TVA Board and NRC approval; further spend \"determined based on future project development\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a28"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F9: \"DOE awarded TVA $400M in December 2025\". CORRECTED: per TVA's 10-Q for the quarter to 30 Jun 2026 (filed 4 Aug 2026), TVA was selected to ENTER NEGOTIATIONS for an approximate $400M grant, with fund allocation and terms still to come. DOE's own page uses \"selection\" and \"awards\" (A30). Error source: secondary relay of DOE's headline. The 10-Q still carries no project cost; TVA contributes up to $93M to GVH design costs through 2026; final Supplemental EIS issued April 2026.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a29"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F9: DOE page (2 Dec 2025): TVA $400,000,000 and Holtec $400,000,000 within \"up to $800 million in federal cost-shared funding\"; no cost-share ratio and no project total on the page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a30"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U104: SACE (advocacy) relays testimony by an energy analyst in Colorado PUC Proceeding 24A-0442E: \"TVA's recent filings\" give $17,949/kW overnight, 2024 dollars, base construction only. Third-hand; the exhibit link found was 403 and the TVA filing was not opened. Same blog gives NuScale $6,833 to $20,130/kW, which does not match IEEFA's $9,964 to $21,561 (A13); $20,130 equals $9.3B / 462 MW, i.e. a financing-inclusive total (our arithmetic). ⏭ Superseded by T1-1 and T1-12: the figure, dollar-year and basis match the draft 2025 IRP's Table 3-3 exactly, which is its almost certain origin; TVA's table labels it a generic first-of-a-kind light-water SMR, not \"the BWRX-300\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a31"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G5/G8: the vendor's current BWRX-300 General Description (GE Vernova Hitachi, Revision J, June 2026), served at the old fact-sheet URLs, publishes NO price: the \"Overall Build Project Costs Estimate or Range\" row is empty; it promises under 30 months first nuclear concrete to fuel load at nth-of-a-kind. The often-cited $2,250/kW NOAK target (2018-2020 fact sheets) could not be opened at a primary (the URLs now serve this document), so it is UNVERIFIED.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a32"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G9/G15: EIA Capital Cost and Performance Characteristics (AEO2025), overnight, 2023 dollars: generic 6x80 MW SMR (480 MW net) $8,936/kW; 2xAP1000 brownfield $7,861/kW; CCGT 2x2x1 $868/kW; CCGT with 95% capture $2,365/kW. An independent government benchmark, but a generic design, not a BWRX-300, and EIA's CCGT predates the turbine squeeze Lazard prices at $2,400-2,600/kW.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a33"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G13: the Colorado PUC exhibit (24A-0442E, Hearing Exhibit 1701) and TVA's draft 2025 / preliminary-final 2026 IRP, the only places a TVA-derived SMR cost is said to sit, were not opened (exhibit link 403; tva.com 403). TVA's 10-Q confirms the Preliminary Final 2026 IRP was published in June 2026. Whether it carries an SMR capital cost is UNRESOLVED. ⏭ Resolved by T1-3 and T1-4: it does ($17,263/kW, 2026 dollars, first-of-a-kind, before tax credits), and the final 2026 IRP keeps it; this row's verdict stands as what pass A could establish.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-a34"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F2: Darlington unit 1's licence, first hold point and operating-licence application. Licence to construct issued April 2025 and valid to 31 March 2035. First of three hold points removed 30 March 2026. Operating-licence application filed 25 March 2026. Primary (the regulator's own page).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F2/U51/U64: Darlington's current schedule and cost. Basemat placed 22 April 2026. The next milestone is the first of eight reactor-building levels, due end-2026. Grid connection targeted end-2030. Unit 1 costs C$6.1B standalone inside a C$20.9B envelope, and C$7.7B has been released for common systems plus unit 1. Approval of units 2-4 is expected in 2028. The \"under budget and ahead of schedule\" line is about the older CANDU refurbishment, not the SMR. Secondary (ANS interview with OPG's chief projects officer, 5 June 2026). The claim that units 2-4 arrive \"between 2033 and 2035\" was NOT found in anything read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U51: Darlington's ORIGINAL target. At selection (WNN, 2 Dec 2021) unit 1 could be completed \"as early as 2028\". The current target is grid connection by end-2030 (B2), a slip of about 2 years in its own first-unit target before any reactor building exists. Secondary (OPG's own 2021 release is behind Cloudflare).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G17: \"Darlington's C$7.7B is unchanged in 2026\". OPG's own Q2 2026 results release (13 Aug 2026) gives NO unit 1 cost or schedule figure. It reports a C$700M First Nations investment and continued construction. The MD&A that would carry the budget line could not be opened. One search found no Ontario FAO or Auditor General review of the SMR's cost (absence after one search only).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U45/F14: Akademik Lomonosov's dates, from IAEA PRIS: construction 15 April 2007, first criticality 2 Nov 2018 (unit 1; unit 2 was 8 Nov), grid 19 Dec 2019, commercial operation 22 May 2020. That is about 13 years from start to commercial operation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U45/U88/U100: Lomonosov's cost and original date. Cost reportedly rose from RUB 9bn (2006) to RUB 37bn, more than 300%. It was originally slated for delivery in 2010, so commercial operation in 2020 was about a decade late. Secondary (POWER, 1 July 2015, \"reportedly\"). It is a 2015 figure in roubles, so there is no final cost. Bellona's \"a decade behind by 2018\" was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U46/F14: Lomonosov's load factors. WNA's table, sourced to PRIS, gives unit 1's annual figures as stated (28.8 / 44.7 / 26.4 / 26.6 / 51.6 / 18.8%) and its cumulative figure as 33.1%. But F14's \"about 33%\" is unit 1 only. Unit 2's cumulative figure is 28.7% (B8), so the plant is about 31%. Fix: \"about 30% over 2020-2025\". Error source: the engine quoted one unit for the plant.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U46: PRIS lifetime factors through 2025. The load factor is 33.1% (unit 1) and 28.7% (unit 2), but the availability factor is 66.5% and 62.5%. The reactors were available about twice as often as they generated. That points to output held down by demand on a small isolated grid (Pevek has about 5,000 people, and the plant supplies its heat and power: (World Nuclear News (trade press), \"Floating nuclear power plant 'has boosted air quality'\" (2026-10-30), saved copy)), not mainly by breakdowns. This is my inference from PRIS's own two factors, and the script must not present a 30% load factor as \"it only works a third of the time\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U47/F14: HTR-PM's dates and rating, from PRIS: construction 9 Dec 2012, grid 14 Dec 2021, commercial operation 6 Dec 2023 (WNA says 5 Dec), so about 11 years. PRIS lists net capacity at 150 MWe against a design net of 200 MWe, so the plant is rated below its design. U47's \"12th Five-Year-Plan test operation before 2015\" and \"50-month build\" were not found in a primary. For a dated original target, see B12 (2018).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U87/F14: HTR-PM's annual load factors: 15.6% in 2024 and 43.8% in 2025 (576 GWh), cumulative 29.2%, against a 150 MWe reference. WNA's table, sourced to PRIS. Unlike Lomonosov, availability was also low (20.7% in 2024 and 52% in 2025), so this is a plant that was often unavailable, not one waiting on demand. The Tsinghua figure of \"1.4 billion kWh a year\" was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U87: PRIS's own lifetime load factor for HTR-PM is 36.3% (and 1.07 TWh lifetime generation), not the 29.2% cumulative in WNA's table. The two series differ, probably in base period or reference power. Say \"roughly a third\", not a decimal.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U88/F14: HTR-PM's \"about $5,000/kW, twice expectations\". The figure is real but dated November 2016 and forward-looking (\"will be\"). It is INET's estimate from signed contracts, not an audited final cost, and it was made when commercial operation was expected in mid-to-late 2018. That gives a dated slip from 2018 to Dec 2023, about 5 years. Secondary (NEI, read via r.jina.ai). The INET hope for later units at $2,000-2,500/kW was not checked.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G17/U48/U89 (LATEST, after 21 Oct): Linglong One on 22 October 2026 (China News Service, 23 Oct). The civil works are complete, the equipment is installed, and system commissioning and clean-up are under way. The cold functional test is complete and commissioning continues. Only \"after the tests pass\" will it load fuel, then go critical, connect to the grid and enter commercial operation. So it is not fuelled, not critical and not grid-connected as of 22 Oct 2026. Unlike the 1 Oct report (B14), this report does not repeat a \"within the year\" target. No later report was found through 2 Nov (searches in English and Chinese).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U89: the 1 Oct 2026 China News Service report: hot functional testing under way, \"sprinting toward the target of fuel loading and grid connection within the year\". Confirmed in the original Chinese. It is a target reported by state media, not a CNNC schedule.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U90: PRIS (read directly, 2 Nov 2026) lists LINGLONG-1 as Under Construction: construction 13 July 2021, no criticality, grid or commercial date, 125 MWe gross / 100 MWe net. This replaces the Wikipedia relay. Caveat: PRIS lags (B16), so it corroborates the status but cannot prove it on its own.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b15"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G17 (method check): PRIS is not current for Chinese units. On 2 Nov 2026 PRIS still shows the neighbouring CHANGJIANG-3 (Hualong One) as Under Construction with no grid date. WNN reports that it went critical on 10 July, connected on 1 Aug and has entered commercial operation ((World Nuclear News (trade press), \"Third unit at Changjiang site commissioned\" (2026-09-17), saved copy)). Never cite PRIS alone for \"Linglong has not connected\", and do not confuse Changjiang-3's 2026 milestones with Linglong One's.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b16"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U49/U48: first concrete 13 July 2021, with a planned 58-month build, which points to about May 2026 (my arithmetic). WNN of 14 Oct 2026 reports hot tests \"with the aim of loading fuel into the reactor within the year\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b17"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U49: the missed \"commercial operation in first half 2026\" target. NucNet (7 Oct 2026) relays that a CNNC official said in Dec 2025 that commercial operation was expected in H1 2026. That target is now missed (B13). The dossier's attribution (\"CNNC's research arm told Reuters\") was not seen in any saved text, so attribute it as \"a CNNC official, as reported in December 2025\". NucNet itself calls the hot-test reports \"unconfirmed reports in local media\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b18"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U50: the 2019 plan, relayed by WNN (22 July 2019) from Nuclear World: first concrete 31 Dec 2019 and a 65-month build to start-up by 31 May 2025. Actual first concrete came about 18 months later (13 July 2021). On start-up, as of 2 Nov 2026 the unit is about 17 months past the 2019 start-up date and counting, not \"at least 18, probably more than 19\". The dossier's figure is a projection, not a measurement. Say \"more than a year and a half late on its first pour, and well over a year past its planned start-up\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b19"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U53/F14: CAREM-25. First concrete Feb 2014. Halted Nov 2019 (by contractor Techint). New contract Nov 2021. CNEA (Oct 2022) expected criticality by end-2027. Remaining work halted Sept 2024 amid budget cuts and layoffs. Correction: the dossier's \"completion end of 2018 (as of 2016)\" conflicts with WNA, which says the target at the Sept 2016 balance-of-plant contract was end-November 2021. Error source: the engine's finding (secondary relay). \"About 85% complete\" (union reps) and the Jan 2025 \"US$200-300M still needed\" were NOT verified.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b20"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U53: PRIS gives CAREM25's construction date as 25 Aug 2015, not Feb 2014 (WNA's first concrete), and still lists it as \"Under Construction\" with no suspension date, despite the Sept 2024 halt. Quote \"construction began in 2014\" only as \"first concrete 2014\". PRIS does not record the stoppage.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b21"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F15/U52/U56: the Natrium fuel delay. On 15 Dec 2022 TerraPower said operation would be delayed at least two years from the proposed 2028 in-service date, because Russia's invasion removed \"the only commercial source of HALEU fuel\". This was the first public fuel-driven delay. Secondary (WNN quoting the CEO). The March 2022 \"Tenex 2025\" and May 2024 \"keeps us up at night\" quotes were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b22"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U52/F15: Kemmerer's current target and permit. TerraPower's own release (4 March 2026) says the project \"is expected to be completed in 2030\" and that the NRC set an initial 27-month review (completed in 18). 4 March was the Commission's VOTE. DOE's notice of the issued permit is dated 9 March ((U.S. Department of Energy, Office of Nuclear Energy, \"NRC Issues Construction Permit for TerraPower's Natrium Advanced Reactor\" (2026-03-09), saved copy)), so \"issued 4 March\" is imprecise: say \"approved in March 2026\". \"Commercial operation 2031\" and \"finish construction by February 2031\" were not found in any TerraPower or NRC text read. Use \"completion targeted for 2030\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b23"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U52: DOE's notice of the Kemmerer permit is dated 9 March 2026, the first NRC construction permit for a commercial non-light-water power reactor. This supports the \"March 9\" side of the dossier's March 4 vs March 9 trap.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b24"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U41/F15: nuclear construction began 23 April 2026, with about 1,600 workers mobilising, about 250 permanent staff and 345 MW. Primary (TerraPower). The 2021 \"about 2,000 at peak\" figure was not verified, so \"the workforce shrank\" is unproven. Present 1,600 as the current figure only.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b25"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U91/F15: the Bechtel exit (WNN, 22 Sept 2026, quoting BOTH companies' spokespeople, so it is no longer effectively single-source; Cowboy State Daily, Power Engineering and NEI also covered it). Corrections: (1) \"Bechtel layoffs of about 200 begin mid-November\" is wrong. Bechtel filed a WARN notice covering about 200 positions, with reductions \"on or about\" 16 Nov, and said it does not expect any actual layoffs because staff move to other projects. (2) \"After failing to agree terms\" is not either company's wording. Both say they \"decided to move forward separately\". TerraPower says the schedule holds and it will rebid the EPC contract, and Bechtel stays to finish the Sodium Test & Fill Facility. Error source: the engine's paraphrase of secondary relays. The \"also built Vogtle 3 and 4\" detail was not checked.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b26"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G17 (Kemmerer since mid-October): on 13 Oct 2026 TerraPower had still not named a replacement lead contractor, and Kemmerer's housing plans for up to 1,600 workers were \"in limbo\". Secondary (local press). No successor was found through 2 Nov (one search).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b27"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F15: Project Pele's slip from 2026 to \"likely 2027 or 2028\" is real but single-source (East Idaho News, 2 Dec 2025, read via r.jina.ai), and the cause it gives is the federal government shutdown, not fuel or technology. It began in 2018. Do not file Pele under \"fuel was the cause\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b28"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F15: Pele's earlier dated target. As of 25 Sept 2024 (WNN), the reactor was to reach INL in 2026 and could generate \"as early as 2026\". BWXT was contracted June 2022. Search snippets suggest even earlier delivery targets (about 2 years from the 2022 contract, and \"2025\" in 2023 coverage), but these were not saved or verified.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b29"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U42: NuScale's FY2025 10-K. Long-lead orders placed for the first twelve upper vessels, forging manufacturing \"has progressed\" at Doosan, and 428 full-time employees at 31 Dec 2025, all confirmed. Correction: the \"plan to cut 154 (28%)\" was announced 8 January 2024. It is a past reduction, not a pending cut against the 2025 headcount. Error source: the engine's reading of the 10-K. CFPP was terminated by a release agreement dated 7 Nov 2023 (confirmed). The Feb 2026 \"first NPM\" release was not checked.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b30"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U61: Doicesti's FID (12 Feb 2026, six NuScale modules, 462 MWe) is conditional. The conditions were given to investors only under a confidentiality agreement and are unpublished, and if any one is unmet the project is deemed unfeasible. The target is operation \"at the beginning of the next decade\". Secondary (POWER, 25 Feb 2026, quoting shareholder documents).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b31"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U62: Wylfa. Contract signed 13 April 2026 for three Rolls-Royce SMRs of at least 1,400 MW. It is a \"firm contract\" covering site-specific design and long-lead ordering, with £2.6bn allocated by government. Two refinements: (1) the NWF loan of up to £599m is to finalise the GENERIC design and goes to Rolls-Royce SMR, not to the Wylfa build. (2) The source read does not say \"pre-decision\" or \"FID still to come\". That wording is the engine's, plausible but unverified here. \"470 MW per unit\" is also not in this source.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b32"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U99: Wloclawek. The 25 June 2026 city agreement covers up to six BWRX-300s and deals with communication, education and information. The project company holds a decision-in-principle, and its studies lead toward a building-permit application that has not yet been filed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b33"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U67/F25: Wood Mackenzie (Q1 2025): the unrisked pipeline of 47 GW counts all announced projects, with 2.5 GW under construction or in development (1.2 GW in Canada). The 5% is the finder's arithmetic, and \"in development\" is broader than \"being built\". The data are about 18 months old.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b34"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U66/F25: the LBNL Queued Up 2024 primary (replacing the Utility Dive relay). 14% of capacity (about 20% of projects) entering queues 2000-2018 reached commercial operation by end-2023. Gas 31%, wind 20%, solar 13% and battery 11%. The report's own summary slide gives solar as 14%, so say \"about 13-14%\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b35"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U39/F25: US offshore wind (DOE market report, 2024 edition). The pipeline is 80,523 MW, with three fully operational projects as of 31 May 2024 including the 132 MW South Fork. The 174 MW installed figure and the \"0.2%\" ratio were not verified (finder's arithmetic). The pipeline is \"potential generating capacity\" including lease areas, so it is not the same kind of denominator as Wood Mackenzie's announced projects.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b36"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U72/F25: CCS (Global CCS Institute, an advocacy body, figures as at 31 July 2026). 113 operating, 59 in construction, 308 in advanced development and 352 in early development, so 113 of 832 is about 14% by count (my arithmetic). The Institute itself warns that capacity is not actual capture.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b37"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U103/F25: the 2007-09 renaissance. 13 companies applied for 31 reactors (the dossier says 30), and two were built, Vogtle 3 (commercial 31 July 2023) and Vogtle 4 (29 April 2024), not \"Vogtle Unit 3\" alone. V.C. Summer 2-3 were cancelled. Tertiary (Wikipedia, the US article). NRC pages were blocked, so say \"about 30 reactors applied for, two built\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b38"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F10/U5: CPAR-2 sets earliest completion 1 Oct 2031, latest 30 Sep 2032, and the permit expires on the latest date. Read in the permit itself. 1 Oct 2031 is an earliest allowed completion date, not a forecast of operation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U106/F10: construction only; operation needs a full FSAR, a safety finding on the final design, Part 51 findings and Price-Anderson proof. Conditions D (geologic mapping), E (updated ETE if an offsite EPZ is required) and F (one-time valve examination) are all in the permit. Nuance: finding 1.D is that the APPLICANT \"has not identified\" any feature needing R&D. The dossier's \"the NRC found no safety feature needing R&D\" overstates it. Say: \"the NRC found the applicant had identified no safety feature needing research and development.\"","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U6/F10: docketed 9 July 2025. The deadline for petitions to intervene was 15 Sep 2025. Docketing to permit (28 Sep 2026) is 446 days, about 14.6 months (my arithmetic).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U6: TVA filed in two parts, 28 Apr and 20 May 2025. First filing to permit is about 17.0 months (my arithmetic). The final SEIS was issued 6 Apr 2026, and the ROD rests on the mandatory hearing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U6/G16: the permit is immediately effective. The notice lists the staff safety evaluation, dated 24 June 2026. The final SEIS is dated 1 April 2026. The ESP-006 permit is dated 19 Dec 2019.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U6/G16: the \"14 months, ahead of schedule\" wording is the NRC's own release, not a summariser's. The NRC chairman's claim is \"four months ahead of schedule\". The release also says an operating licence is still needed. These are the agency's own framing and must be attributed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G3/G16: no one petitioned against the Clinch River permit. The August 2026 hearing was the uncontested (mandatory) one, held 13 Aug 2026 in Oak Ridge.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U78/F10: Kemmerer (Natrium, CPAR-1) was submitted 28 Mar 2024, docketed 21 May 2024, final SE Dec 2025, hearing 4 Mar 2026, permit decision 9 Mar 2026. That is 21.6 months from docketing and 23.4 from submittal (my arithmetic). This resolves the dossier's \"March 4 vs March 9\" conflict: 4 March was the HEARING and 9 March the DECISION. WNN's \"18 months\" plausibly counts docketing to final SE (about 18.5 months).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U92 (Vogtle part, upgraded from DOE secondary to the NRC page): application 28 Mar 2008, docketed 30 May 2008, final SER 5 Aug 2011, mandatory hearing 27-28 Sep 2011, COLs issued 10 Feb 2012. That is about 44.4 months from docketing (my arithmetic). Not like-for-like: a COL licenses construction AND operation of two large units. The NuScale part of U92 was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G11/G16 (pro-safety evidence, the NRC's own words): the staff found reasonable assurance that the final design will conform with adequate margin for safety. The passive-safety description is TVA's summary as recorded by the NRC, so attribute it to the applicant: Safety Category 1 functions need no outside power or operator action for 72 hours after a design-basis accident.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G11/G16 (new, load-bearing): NO emergency planning zone was adopted at the permit stage. TVA kept two options from the ESP, a site-boundary EPZ (no offsite monitoring or protective-action planning) or a 2-mile EPZ, and chooses at the operating licence. It did not submit the accident-dose analyses that size the EPZ. FEMA's 2018 finding covers only the 2-mile boundary's local factors and expressly does not endorse its adequacy.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G11/G16: the ACRS letter (20 June 2026, ML26058A279) supports the permit, calls the PSAR sufficiently complete, and lists open safety concerns left for the operating licence and startup: natural-circulation stability at startup and low power, isolation-condenser performance, and beyond-design-basis \"practical elimination\" claims.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G3: NRC v. Texas (decided 18 June 2025, 6-3): non-parties to an NRC licensing proceeding cannot get judicial review of the licence. My inference, flagged: since no one intervened at Clinch River (C7), no outside group has an obvious route to challenge this permit in court.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G3: court challenges to the 2026 rules (Part 53, July 16, Sept 11, Oct 22, Oct 30) or to EO 14300. Four targeted searches found NONE. The 11 AGs filed comments urging withdrawal, not a lawsuit. A pro-industry suit exists the other way (Texas, Utah and Last Energy v. NRC, E.D. Tex. 6:24-cv-00507, filed Dec 2024, seeking LESS regulation of microreactors; per secondary reports more states joined in Apr 2025). Its status was not found. No court docket was checked, so this is \"none found\", not \"none\".","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-c14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U7/F11: Part 53 was published 30 Mar 2026 (91 FR 15696), effective 29 Apr 2026. It responds to NEIMA, and the NRC says EO 14300 \"will complement\" it. Not checked: the 25-26 March affirmation, and the staff's \"18 months or less\" design-review expectation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c15"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U8/U12/F11: EO 14300 (23 May 2025) sets proposed rules in 9 months and final rules in 18. It sets a separate 18-month cap per new-reactor application, counted FROM THE FIRST REQUIRED STEP. Measured that way Clinch River took about 17 months (first filing to permit), not 14. It also orders reductions in force, an ACRS cut to the \"minimum necessary\" and reconsideration of LNT. The FR citation 90 FR 22587 was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c16"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U9/F11: the September overhaul is a PROPOSED rule, published 24 Sep 2026 (91 FR 60702-60790), with comments due 9 Nov 2026. It is \"one effort\" under EO section 5. Projected savings are $311M-$411M over 30 years, and the final rule is planned for late 2026. It removes the 15-year expiry for standard design approvals. \"339-page\" was not checked (the FR span is 89 pages).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c17"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U10/F11: the DOE/DOW test-data rule was published 26 Oct 2026 and takes effect 25 Nov 2026 (not in force on 2 Nov). It addresses EO section 5(d). The NRC says it delegates nothing and forgoes no independent review. The 13-group objection was not retrieved.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c18"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U79: the NRC release of 30 Oct 2026: three tracks; most cases in about a year, the simplest in about six months; license standards unchanged; effective 30 days after FR publication.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c19"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U79/F11: two errors. (1) FR status is out of date: the final rule (FR doc 2026-22443) went on public inspection 2 Nov 2026 for publication 3 Nov 2026, per the Federal Register public-inspection record for that document (saved copy). So it takes effect about 3 Dec 2026 (my arithmetic) and applies to proceedings noticed after that. (2) The \"18 / 12 / 6-month tracks\" (ANS relay) are wrong as hearing durations. In the rule's own table, 18 and 12 months are the NRC's CURRENT REVIEW SCHEDULES. The hearing-completion dates are 262 days (Standard) and 217 days (Focused), or 409 and 364 days when a late contention is added, and 147-162 days for expedited cases. Use the NRC's words: \"about a year\" and \"roughly six months\". The error came from a secondary relay (ANS).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c20"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U11/F12: 11 AGs (IL, CA, NM, WA, CO, OR, DE, MD, MN, VT, MA) commented on 31 Aug 2026 on the JULY proposal (NRC-2025-0975), not on Clinch River. They argue: \"coordination descriptions\" could replace binding offsite plans; costs could shift to states; siting could move to denser areas; every extension request was denied; 27 rulemakings were listed as of 30 Aug. They urge withdrawal.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c21"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U14/U80/F12: GAO (28 Sep 2026): a net loss of about 500 staff, July 2024 to June 2026 (about 740 separations, 270 hires). NEXUS is due Q1 FY2027. There are no metrics, and there are two recommendations. Nuance: GAO frames the ADVANCE Act authorities as three AREAS (direct hire; compensation flexibility; bonuses for hiring and performance). The NRC used only PERFORMANCE bonuses (18 bonuses, $335,000) and not hiring bonuses. \"Used one of three authorities\" is loose. It is a workforce audit and says nothing on review quality.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c22"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U15/F12: OIG FY2026 challenges (OIG-NRC-26-M-01, 12 Dec 2025). Challenge #1 is reform with integrity. The OIG warns that untimely reviews could delay deployment. The challenges \"do not necessarily indicate problems\". No audit finding on review quality.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c23"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U54/F12 (upgraded to the primary budget justification): the FY2027 request is $892.3M, -8.1% vs FY2026 enacted, with 2,606 FTE (-7.0%, -196). The 30/40-FTE licensing/oversight split was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c24"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U54 (secondary, ANS report of the May 2026 Senate hearing): the chairman cites \"above-average attrition\" but says the mission is delivered; a commissioner says 500+ lost and only 59 added in 16 months; a senator says \"shrinking your ability to conduct oversight\"; a commissioner warns that concurrent applications will be harder. The 59 does not match GAO's 270 hires (different windows and definitions), so never combine them.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c25"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U55/F12 (secondary trade press, quoting the NRC spokesperson and the commissioner's own statement): a commissioner was terminated on 13 June 2025 \"without cause\" (his words). This is a dated official act, so a name is allowed; the editor decides. NOT checked: the June 2025 resignation, Dec 2025 quorum, June 2026 reconfirmation and Trump v. Slaughter parts of U55.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c26"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U71/F12 (advocacy; UCS blog dated 10 Sep 2026): April 2026 Commission decision to replace NRC-run force-on-force tests with observing licensee exercises by 2028; security inspection resources cut \"by over fifty percent in some cases\"; new owners can credit local police instead of their own force; the security office was eliminated. Nuance: the \"June 23, 2026 proposed security rule\" date is not in this post, so it is unverified here. The same post says 5-10% of plants still fail force-on-force each year. All are UCS's claims and must be attributed.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c27"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U83: the NRC's 23 Sep 2026 deck is about research/test-reactor and Category II/III MATERIAL security, cites EO 14300, and sets the final rule for Jan 2027. It is not commercial SMR plant security, so do not use it for reactor security.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c28"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U13/F12: the 2023 rule (effective 18 Dec 2023) is an ALTERNATIVE EP framework for SMRs and other new technologies. It sizes the EPZ by a 1 rem / 96-hour dose criterion, and with a site-boundary EPZ \"the NRC does not mandate offsite\" radiological planning. The large-LWR roughly 10-mile EPZ quoted is the 1980 rule this alternative sits beside. NOT verified: UCS's same-day criticism, FEMA's 2020 concerns, and the 3-1 dissent in 2019.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c29"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F13/U1-U4: power levels confirmed from DOE's own slide deck. Mark-0 <100 W thermal (4 June); Unity <1 W, cold (30 June); Aalo <10 W, dry (4 July); Ward 250 zero-power criticality 18 June and \"full power 100 kWt\" on 2 July. CORRECTION: F13's \"None produced electricity\" is wrong. DOE's slide says Ward 250's 2 July run involved \"electricity generation\" to power an NVIDIA Spark. Secondary reports (not saved, unverified) say a thermoelectric generator ran a desktop AI computer. That is a demonstration-scale trickle, not grid power. The deck's own selections slide lists Ward 250 at about 250 kWt, so the rating conflict is inside DOE's own deck (100 kWt \"full power\" vs ~250 kWt design). The error came from the engine finding, which dropped its own U4 detail.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c30"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U3/F13: the DOE release (6 July 2026) counts four, says the President asked for three, and calls Aalo's \"test reactor, Aalo-X\" a \"zero-power fueled criticality demonstration\". \"Early Saturday\" is 4 July. U3's \"not yet Aalo-X itself\" contradicts DOE's naming. Accurate wording: DOE calls it Aalo-X, but its own slide shows a <10 W critical assembly, and the 30 MWt full-power test comes in 2027.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c31"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G7: Clinch River (TVA, the peg project, one BWRX-300) runs on ordinary LEU, not HALEU. NRC's final SEIS (April 2026): uranium dioxide fuel under 5% enriched, 240 GNF2 assemblies, 45 MTU first core, a fuel type like today's reactors. The HALEU bottleneck does NOT apply to the peg reactor.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G7: Darlington (OPG, one BWRX-300, Canada) uses the same GNF2 UO2 fuel at 3.81% average and 4.95% maximum enrichment (OPG's own licence-to-construct submission to the CNSC, June 2024). Ordinary LEU, not HALEU. The CNSC construction licence does not authorise fuel on site; fuel comes at the operating licence.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G7: NuScale US460 (the 77 MWe VOYGR module, standard design approval May 2025) uses UO2 pellets of at most 4.95% U-235, according to the NRC's final safety evaluation, chapter 4. Ordinary LEU, not HALEU.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G7: Natrium (Kemmerer Unit 1, TerraPower) needs HALEU. NRC's final EIS (NUREG-2268): metallic fuel enriched between 5 and 20 wt%; TerraPower asked for an exemption from the 5 wt% limit and has not publicly given a maximum enrichment. The DOE-lab paper (D22) models 16.5%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G7: Xe-100 (X-energy; Long Mott/Dow in Texas; the AEP/Amazon Indiana project in the peg) uses TRISO pebble fuel at 15.5% enrichment, which is HALEU (the Argonne/INL peer-reviewed paper's design table: PWR 4.5, VOYGR 4.95, Natrium 16.5, Xe-100 15.5). The NRC's Long Mott notice says only that it uses TRISO fuel, so the 15.5% comes from this DOE-lab table, not an NRC finding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G7: Kairos Hermes (35 MWt test reactor, Oak Ridge; construction permit Dec 2023) uses TRISO pebbles containing HALEU, enriched to various levels below 20%, according to the NRC's final safety evaluation. It is a test reactor, not a power plant.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G6: Who takes SMR waste. Under the Nuclear Waste Policy Act the federal government is responsible for permanent disposal and the generators pay. A new reactor shows it is negotiating a DOE disposal contract (section 302(b)); for Kemmerer the NRC accepted documentation of good-faith negotiation as compliance at the construction-permit stage. Caveat: this is the NRC's DRAFT safety evaluation, chapter 1, released for the advisory committee (ACRS); the final SE was not opened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G6: Where Clinch River's spent fuel goes: the pool holds eight years of spent fuel plus a full core; after that it goes into dry casks in an on-site storage installation (ISFSI). The NRC used Yucca Mountain only as a stand-in destination for its transport analysis. There is no operating repository, so on the record the fuel stays at the Clinch River site.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G6: Who pays meanwhile. DOE's own July 2025 deck: the Judgment Fund (taxpayers) had paid $11.1 billion as of FY2024 for DOE's failure to take spent fuel, and the estimated remaining liability net of payments was $37.6 billion in FY2024. About 95,000 t of commercial spent fuel exists today, and up to about 180,000 t by the end of the current fleet. The Nuclear Waste Fund balance appears only as a chart, with no figure we could quote.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G6: Canada's contrast. Darlington's used fuel goes to the pools, then to dry storage on site. Canada's waste agency (NWMO) told the CNSC that its planned deep geological repository near Ignace could take BWRX-300 used fuel, but only if the host community is willing. So Canada has a sited repository plan and the US has none.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F16: HALEU is badly short. DOE's January 2025 Record of Decision confirms \"more than 40 MT by 2030\" and that no sufficient domestic commercial enrichment capability exists. The claim's \"up to 50 t/yr by 2035\" is NOT in this ROD (unverified; possibly an older DOE estimate). Centrus is the only enricher, but DOE also makes small amounts by downblending HEU, so \"the only producer\" should read \"the only enricher\". The claim's scope is also wrong: HALEU applies to Natrium, Xe-100 and Kairos, NOT to BWRX-300 or NuScale (D1-D3).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U18: DOE demand. 40+ t by 2030 confirmed. WRONG: \"up to 50 metric tons per year by 2035\" and the wording \"HALEU is not available from domestic suppliers\" are not in the cited ROD; the ROD says only \"additional amounts required each year thereafter\". Right wording: \"DOE projects more than 40 tonnes needed by 2030, and more each year after.\" Error source: engine finding (the figure was attributed to a source that doesn't contain it).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U16: Centrus's demonstration cascade. DOE split the next option into 3 months of maintenance and storage with no production, says it does not currently intend to exercise further options, and the FY2027 budget request does not fund the cascade. The demonstration rate was 900 kg of HALEU UF6 per production year.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U17 + U95: the $900M contract's deliverable. WRONG in U17: \"options for 5 more MTU\". Right: two DOE options of 5 MTU each, up to 10 MTU more, at $17M per MTU. WRONG in U95: delivery \"by March 2032\". Right: the 10-Q says by July 2032. Error source: U17 is the engine's summary; U95 is a secondary relay (POWER).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U17/U95: 12 t/yr is the initial build-out's capacity target, with first new capacity by 2029. More than 1,900 kg had been produced cumulatively by mid-June 2026. Capacity is not output.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d15"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U19: Russian LEU ban (Pub. L. 118-62). It starts 90 days after enactment, allows waivers on two grounds, caps waived volumes each year 2024-2027, ends all waivers by Jan 1, 2028, and the ban itself ends Dec 31, 2040.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d16"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U20: Russia's own export restriction (Nov 14, 2024), now extended through Dec 31, 2027, requires a licence for each shipment. Through 2027 well over half of Centrus's LEU deliveries come from Tenex, and its other sources cannot replace that. The third waiver request was filed Dec 11, 2024, and it is uncertain whether waivers will come or come in time. The decree number (1516) and its Oct 2, 2025 date are not in the 10-Q and were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d17"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U21: Russia's share of US enrichment purchases. OUT OF DATE: EIA Table 16 now includes 2025. Russia's share was 27.2% in 2023 (4,141 of 15,240 thousand SWU) and 20.1% in 2024 (3,043 of 15,159), then rose to 25.8% in 2025 (3,284 of 12,710). Russia was the largest single country source in 2024 and 2025 (withheld rows are too small to beat it). The 2024 shares for France 17.6%, the Netherlands 14.8% and the US 19.1% are correct. Right wording: \"about a quarter of the enrichment US reactor owners bought in 2025 was Russian, the largest single source\". Error source: engine finding (it read the table before the 2025 column was added).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d18"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F17: Russian-fuel risk reaches ordinary light-water SMRs through standard LEU enrichment. The law and the Tenex dependence are confirmed (D16, D17); the Russian share is corrected per D18 (25.8% in 2025, not \"fell to 20.1%\"). The waiver cutoff (Jan 1, 2028) ends the waivers, not the ban.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d19"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U22 (abstract-sourced sample): PNAS 2022 (Krall, Macfarlane, Ewing). \"Factors of 2 to 30\" is the abstract's own wording, across three designs and several waste classes. Spent-fuel volume: NuScale 160 MWth 5.1, IMSR 11, Toshiba 4S 2.0, against 2.0 m3/GWth-y for a PWR (2.6x, 5.5x, 1.0x). The text gives NuScale's long-lived decommissioning waste as 9 to 17 times higher. The dossier's \"long-lived up to ~30x, short-lived up to ~35x\" comes from the paper's radar chart and is not stated in the text, so it is unverified.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d20"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U23: PNAS radiotoxicity and fissile content. iPWR spent fuel (burnup 33 MWd/kg, against 50 for a PWR) is about 50% more radiotoxic at 10,000 years. Fissile content is 2.3 wt% (iPWR), 17 wt% (4S) and 2.1 wt% (IMSR), against 1.3 wt% for a PWR. SMRs do not reduce the mobile fission products. The paper ALSO says volume is not the most important metric, a line the episode should carry for fairness.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d21"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U24: Argonne/INL paper (Kim, Boing, Dixon; Nuclear Engineering and Technology 56, 2024, online 31 Jan 2024). WRONG: \"Natrium and Xe-100 far less spent-fuel mass (0.58x and 0.25x)\". Right: Natrium mass 0.28x (6.01 against 21.7 t/GWe-yr); 0.58x is Natrium's spent-fuel VOLUME. Also, VOYGR mass is reported inconsistently: the table gives 23.9 against 21.7 (a 1.10x ratio) but prints the ratio as 1.23, and the text says 20% more. Say \"slightly more (10-20%)\". Confirmed: VOYGR volume 1.08x, Xe-100 volume 12.3x, VOYGR GTCC decommissioning waste 6x. \"No major challenges\" is CONDITIONAL on optimised waste management. Error source: engine finding (column misread).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d22"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U82: Argonne/INL radiotoxicity at 10,000 years: VOYGR 1.06x, Natrium 1.47x (more Pu-239 from breeding), Xe-100 0.34x. Differences have limited impact on repository isolation. The paper models the 250 MWt / 77 MWe VOYGR core, while PNAS models the 160 MWth core, so the two are not head-to-head.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d23"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F18: \"waste is disputed and design-dependent\" is fair. One figure is corrected per D22: Natrium spent-fuel MASS is about 0.28x (the paper says reduced by a factor of 3.6), and its volume is 0.58x. The paper itself confirms the 42%-smaller volume. Neither study covers the BWRX-300.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d24"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U57: NuScale's rebuttal, read through POWER (secondary; the letter itself was not opened). NuScale's technology chief told the PNAS editor there was a factual error and cited the 250 MWt core (about 45,000 MWd/t average, 62 GWd/MTHM maximum). The authors replied that the 160 MWth choice was deliberate. The PNAS text uses 33 MWd/kg for the iPWR, not the dossier's \"about 34\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d25"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U58: Kemp et al., Science (June 2024), secondary-verified via a Reuters wire copy. Science.org was blocked, so the paper was not opened. Confirmed: directly weapons-usable; 1,000 kg or less of 19.75% HALEU for a Hiroshima-scale bomb; a 10-12% cap would be \"far safer with only modest costs\". NOT found in the saved wire copy: the NNSA administrator's reply, which stays unverified.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d26"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U25: NRC proposed rule (FR June 26, 2026; comments closed July 27, 2026). It makes Category II rules (10 CFR 73.67) largely performance-based, switches \"early\" to \"prompt\" detection, and the NRC links it to HALEU fuel for new reactors. NOT verified for Category II: \"existing licensees need not change\". The \"not required to modify\" text I found refers to section 73.55 power-reactor licensees.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d27"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F19: \"regulators are actively loosening, not tightening\" is a characterisation the source only half supports. The NRC frames the change as closing regulatory gaps with a standardised Category II approach, which adds requirements such as compensatory measures and defence in depth, while also moving from prescriptive to performance-based rules. Say \"rewriting the rules to be more flexible\", not \"loosening\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d28"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U26: Brookhaven 2021. It is a conference presentation (slides for the April 2021 Advanced Reactors Safeguards and Security Conference), not a formal study. The content is confirmed: HALEU is Category II, the rules were last updated 1979, decisions are case by case, and fresh HALEU at a reactor \"may not be adequately protected\". Describe it as \"a Brookhaven lab presentation\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d29"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U96: A safeguards analyst's paper (VCDNP, 8 Aug 2024): there is no practical technology to reprocess TRISO fuel now, so it is seen as low proliferation risk; going from 19.75% to 90% takes a little over 40% of the enrichment effort needed from 5% LEU.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d30"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U81: The NNSA received 1.7 t of HALEU from Japan (announced May 7, 2026), to be reconstituted at Y-12. The three DOE allocation rounds and the Kairos Hermes contract are NOT on this page and were not verified here.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d31"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U84: DOE Tier 2 awards: more than $94M to eight companies, and all eight amounts are confirmed. These awards are not for reactor builds; the $800M Tier 1 (Dec 2025) went to TVA and Holtec. The exact May 14, 2026 date was not confirmed on the saved page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d32"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U94: WRONG: Urenco's NRC approval to enrich to 10% is \"below the bottom of the HALEU 5-20% range\". Right: 10% is INSIDE the HALEU range (above 5%, below 20%; see D4's NRC definition), but well short of the 19.75% that DOE procures and most advanced designs use. Confirmed: General Matter construction in 2026 and operation by 2034; Orano's task order is for LEU, with production from 2031. Error source: engine finding.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d33"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U27 / F20: Amazon's X-energy \"5 GW\" deal is options and priority rights, not a purchase commitment: first-priority manufacturing slots for 2031-2039, a right of first refusal, most-favoured pricing, and payments to Amazon tied to cost efficiencies against the initial projects' PPA prices, which X-energy calls potentially material. Read from the final prospectus dated April 23, 2026. No price figure appears in the parts read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U28: Energy Northwest (Cascade) rests on a development-and-funding agreement, 4 x Xe-100 = 320 MWe, upsizable to 960 MWe. Offtake by Dow and Amazon is \"expected\", not contracted. Dow can walk away for convenience. X-energy's revenue today is mostly government contract work (ARDP).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U29 / G4: Oklo-Switch \"12 GW\" is a December 2024 Master Power Agreement. In the June 2026 10-Q Oklo still lists Switch, and also Meta, among \"prospective customers\", and its plan is to turn nonbinding agreements into binding PPAs. Equinix, Diamondback and Prometheus are non-binding LOIs. The Meta item is a January 5, 2026 prepayment agreement. Six-month net loss $81.6M. No going-concern language was found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U30: Meta's \"6.6 GW\" (Jan 2026) is an \"up to\" sum of new AND existing power. It counts 20-year purchases of more than 2.1 GW from two operating Vistra plants, 433 MW of uprates, TerraPower funding for 2 Natrium units (690 MW, as early as 2032), \"rights for energy\" from up to 6 more Natrium units (2.1 GW, by 2035), and an Oklo campus \"poised to add up to 1.2 GW\". About 2.5 GW of the 6.6 is existing plants and their uprates, not new SMRs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U97 / G10: Google-Kairos-TVA. TVA's own report to Congress confirms that TVA (not Google) signed a PPA for up to 50 MW from Hermes 2, online as early as 2030. WNN ((World Nuclear News (trade press), \"Google, Kairos Power, TVA announce collaboration\" (2025-08-18), saved copy)) adds that Google gets the clean-energy attributes. No price or term appears in either. The same TVA report lists the ENTRA1/NuScale \"up to 6 gigawatts\" as an agreement \"to explore\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G14 / G10 (AEP's own words, Oct 19, 2026): the Indiana deal is a joint development agreement to evaluate FEASIBILITY of 12 x 80 MW Xe-100 (960 MW) at a former coal site in Sullivan County. Amazon gives development funding. Only IF it reaches a final investment decision does Amazon \"intend\" to buy the power under a long-term contract covering the plant's costs. No price, no cost estimate, no date. Not a decision to build.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G14 / G10 (Amazon's own words): Amazon is \"a project development funder\" and \"would be\" the first buyer once the plant runs. It ties this to its White House Ratepayer Protection Pledge. Its jobs claim is \">1,000 construction, >100 permanent\". The pledge is a public commitment; the binding contract terms are not public. Amazon led X-energy's 2024 Series C-1 round, which \"closed at $700 million\" in total; Amazon's own share is not stated. No IURC filing under SEA 424 was found or searched for in this pass.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U32 / F21: on Dec 2, 2025 DOE selected TVA ($400M) and Holtec ($400M): up to $800M cost-shared, from a $900M Gen III+ SMR solicitation issued March 2025. NEW: TVA's award also covers follow-on units with Indiana Michigan Power (AEP's Indiana utility) and Elementl. The page gives no cost-share ratio, no project cost and nothing on ratepayers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G1 / G12 / U101 (TVA's own report to Congress, March 2026): TVA's board has approved only up to $350M to EXPLORE advanced reactors, $251M of it spent by Sept 30, 2025. A decision to build needs a further board approval. The $400M DOE grant was still \"in negotiations\". TVA's board has \"final authority\" on rates (no state commission), and TVA's bonds were $22.1B against a $30B statutory limit. No construction budget, no total cost and no vendor fixed-price term appear. The ~$5.4B / $17,949/kW figures remain unsourced, and this report predates the CP.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U101 / G12: \"the oft-cited ~$5.4B for the first 300 MW unit\". No TVA, NRC, DOE or GE Vernova primary opened in this pass gives a Clinch River cost. TVA's March 2026 report names only the $350M exploratory approval. The 10-K could not be fetched (SEC block). Treat any Clinch River total cost as unverified. ⏭ Superseded by T1-7 and T1-11: TVA's own construction-cost estimate exists, filed with the NRC and withheld as proprietary; the ~$5.4B is press arithmetic on the draft IRP's $/kW assumption. No Clinch River total stays on air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-e10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U33 / F21: ARDP 2020: $80M each to TerraPower and X-energy; $3.2B over seven years \"subject to the availability of future appropriations\", matched by industry. CONFIRMED for 2020. The August 2026 \"up to $1B more\" for Long Mott (cumulative ~$2.15B) and the \"decision likely H1 2027\" are still ANS secondary only; no DOE notice was opened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U85: CORRECTED source description. The cited URL is not an LPO listing but DOE's Oct 27, 2026 release: a CONDITIONAL commitment of up to $5B for Constellation uprates (nearly 1.1 GW), not yet a loan. The Vistra $4.2B, $17.5B AP1000 supply-chain and Crane items are not on this page and were not opened. \"No SMR loan\" remains an absence, not a finding. Error came from the engine finding (it described one release as the office's page).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U85 (part): Duane Arnold restart loan of up to $1.9B to NextEra, financial close Sept 8, 2026, 615 MW. A restart, not an SMR.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U34 / F21: P.L. 119-21 fixes the 45Y \"applicable year\" at 2032 and ends 45Y only for wind/solar placed in service after Dec 31, 2027 (subject to the Act's begin-construction rules, not read here). CORRECTED on the nuclear bonus: the new energy-community test is a METROPOLITAN STATISTICAL AREA with at least 0.17% nuclear-related direct employment. The NRC-permit rule does NOT deem the test met. It deems a facility to have an approved reactor design, i.e. to COUNT as an \"advanced nuclear facility\". 48E's carve-out is right. The 100/75/50/0% phase-down sits in existing 45Y(d), not in this text (Cornell summary unchecked). Error from the engine finding.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U35 / F21: Army Janus (Aug 26, 2026): five vendor-base pairs, up to a combined $2.2B (approx. $2.2B across FY2027-2031), paid only after technical milestones, contractor-owned and operated. CORRECTED on the date: September 2028 is the target for operation of a FIRST reactor, not for five. Error from the engine finding (F21: \"five base microreactors targeting September 2028\").","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e15"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U98: Project Pele. TRISO fuel arrived at INL on Nov 5, 2025 (reported Dec 2, 2025). Launch slipped from 2026 to \"likely 2027 or 2028\", which the article blames on the government shutdown. 1-5 MWe. APPROXIMATE: the article says the reactor \"will be manufactured\" by BWXT, not that it is \"still being manufactured\". This is local news, secondary, not a DoD primary. The Army calls Janus the successor (\"grabbing the baton from Project Pele\", see E15 file).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e16"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U36 / F23 (sample: wording from an ABSTRACT): Sepulveda et al., Joule 2(11) 2018. Nearly 1,000 cases. Firm low-carbon availability cuts costs 10-62% \"across fully decarbonized cases\". Below 50 gCO2/kWh these resources lower costs in the vast majority of cases. \"Firm\" = nuclear, gas with CCS and bioenergy together, so it is NOT nuclear- or SMR-specific. Only the abstract was read (the Cell full text returned 403). The paper's cost inputs and the nuclear-only results were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e17"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U37 / F23: MIT (IEEE PES Magazine summary of the 2018 MIT study). Without nuclear, deep-decarbonisation cost \"increases significantly\", mostly from storage build-out. The nuclear share grows as its cost falls. The modelled 2050 overnight costs were New England $5,500/kW nominal and $4,100/kW improved, T-B-T $2,800/$2,100. These are 2050 large-plant assumptions, not SMR quotes.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e18"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F23: \"DOE's draft modelling shows California 2045 system costs of $80-94/MWh with nuclear against $129-150 without\". CORRECTED attribution: these are three models from Baik et al. (2021), which the DOE draft cites, not DOE's own modelling. Say \"a 2021 study cited by DOE's draft\". Error from the engine finding.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e19"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U40 / F24: DOE 2022 coal-to-nuclear study. 80% of the evaluated (157 retired + 237 operating, screened) coal sites are \"amenable\". 15-35% overnight-capital saving vs greenfield in a 1,200 MWe case study. Up to $275M of regional activity and 650 new permanent regional jobs. The site is explicitly hypothetical. Trap: the abstract's \"924 GWe\" is a typo for MWe.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e20"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U68 / F24 / G18: \"DOE's later guide gives much smaller job numbers (30-100 net direct; 102 permanent for 900 MWe)\". CORRECTED. The 2024 guide (primary, opened) has two measures. (a) Fig. 6, plant-only operating jobs: net gains vs a same-size coal plant of about 31 up to 102 (my reading of the chart labels, 100 to 900 MWe). So 102 is the NET GAIN at 900 MWe, not total permanent jobs. (b) Total long-term impacts (plant + supply chain + spending), excluding construction: added jobs from 65 (smallest plant, smallest community) to 497 (largest plant, 200,000+ population; the table runs 100 to 900 MWe). The 2022 study's 650 (924 MWe replacing 1,200 MWe coal, regional) is the same kind of measure as (b), so DOE did not \"cut\" its number: the measures and cases differ. Error from the secondary relay (Utility Dive, which this pass could not open: 403).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e21"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U69 / F24: Kemmerer is NEAR the Naughton coal plant (not on the coal units) and reuses infrastructure (cooling-water intakes, electrical distribution). WNN 2021: two coal units were due to retire in 2025, a third already converted to a gas peaker. The later decision to convert rather than retire is NOT in this file. The 1,600 here is projected COAL job losses across Wyoming, not Natrium jobs.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e22"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F24: \"Kemmerer's peak construction workforce estimate fell from about 2,000 to about 1,600, with about 250 permanent staff\". APPROXIMATE: TerraPower says it is \"mobilizing\" roughly 1,600 workers to BEGIN construction, not that 1,600 is the peak. The earlier ~2,000 was not checked. 250 full-time staff confirmed.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e23"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U59 / F22 / G14 (statute text, SEA 424 enrolled, 2025): before a certificate, a utility may petition the IURC to approve SMR project development costs. It recovers 80% through a rate adjustment over the lesser of the incurral period or 3 years, and defers 20% to the next rate case. Costs over the best estimate need a \"reasonable, necessary, and prudent\" finding. CORRECTED on cancelled projects: costs of a cancelled project are recoverable if prudently incurred but WITHOUT A RETURN, unless the IURC also finds good cause, offsets (DOE money, third-party revenue, or reimbursement by the customer who asked for it) and that a return avoids harm. The offsets are conditions for earning a return, not a general netting. \"Public Law 48\" was not seen in the text. Error from the secondary relay (law-firm bulletin, as summarised by the engine).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e24"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"U108 / F22: Indiana critics (advocacy, reported by trade press). Consumer advocates say the law puts the cost on ratepayers. The Citizens Action Coalition's director objects to ratepayers footing costs for \"monopoly utility companies and big tech\". The industrial consumers' lobbyist cites overruns. The sponsor amended the bill to widen IURC discretion to deny cancelled or over-budget recovery. Trap: the \"75%\" large-user figure in this article may refer to a companion bill (SEA 423/HEA 1007), not SEA 424.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e25"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"F22: UAMPS/CFPP members had negotiated \"off-ramps\" to quit before issuing bonds for their share. Secondary (Salt Lake Tribune). Pass A holds the UAMPS primaries.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e26"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G4 (grid operator tests the demand claim): PJM's Jan 2026 forecast still grows strongly (summer peak 253,077 MW by 2046, +96,704 MW over 20 years). But it CUT the near term through 2032 vs 2025, partly by vetting large-load (data-centre) requests: near-term years need firm commitments, and later projects are \"non-firm\" and derated. Demand is real but partly speculative. This cuts both ways.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e27"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G4 / G18 (the pro side's firm-capacity need, from the reliability regulator): NERC's 2025 LTRA. 10-yr summer peak growth over 224 GW, 69% above last year's projection, mostly data centres. 13 of 23 areas face adequacy challenges. Data centres are counted only once past speculative stages, but PJM/ERCOT see some large loads \"slowed or failed to materialize\". Utility IRPs in one SERC area list SMRs among new resources. NY's governor directed NYPA to build advanced nuclear (June 23, 2025).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e28"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G18 (baseline): EIA Table 6.07.B, US nuclear capacity factor 91.1% in 2025 (90.8% in 2024) on 98,408.2 MW. This is the EXISTING large-reactor fleet's record, not an SMR figure; wind 2025 was 33.8% and solar PV 24.0% in the same table.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e29"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"G2 (state regulators beyond Indiana): not closed. No docket or statute was opened for Wyoming, Texas, Washington (EFSEC), Michigan PSC (Palisades), Colorado 24A-0442E or Utah. The only extra state item found is New York's executive direction to NYPA (E28), and the AEP Indiana deal shows no IURC filing in AEP's or Amazon's release.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-e30"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"A31/A34/E10: TVA's draft 2025 IRP (Volume 1, dated September 2024; public release 23 Sept 2024) prints the $17,949/kW figure itself, in Table 3-3 \"Overnight Capital Costs\" (repeated in Table E-1). The row is a generic resource option, \"Small Modular Reactor – Light Water (first-of-a-kind)\", at 285 MW summer net dependable capacity, in 2024 dollars, overnight. The nth-of-a-kind option is $12,471/kW. The table doesn't name Clinch River or the BWRX-300 in that row. This is the earliest document found that prints the number.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"The draft IRP ties the nuclear assumption to TVA's Clinch River work and calls it \"all-in\". TVA's own benchmark consultant (Horizons Energy) found the first-of-a-kind SMR cost \"significantly higher than the industry average\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"A34 (was UNRESOLVED): the Preliminary Final 2026 IRP (June 2026) does carry an SMR capital cost. Table 2-4: first-of-a-kind light-water SMR, 285 MW, $17,263/kW in 2026 dollars, overnight, before any tax credit. Nth-of-a-kind falls to $8,743/kW. TVA now describes nuclear costs as \"all-in costs plus risk contingencies\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"The Final 2026 IRP (Volume 1, August 2026) keeps the same $17,263/kW. It assumes costs fall to nth-of-a-kind over the first eight units. Its text ties the light-water SMR option to the BWRX-300 (the Clinch River design). This PDF's text layer drops some characters (T, A, S, 0, 2), so quote it only from the clean lines below.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"Status of the IRP: TVA's NEPA page says the board accepted the final IRP recommendations at its August 2026 meeting. So the brief's \"Preliminary Final\" is now superseded by a final IRP (same SMR figure).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TVA's own Environmental Report for the construction permit (Enclosure 5, Revision 0, cover letter dated 28 April 2025) gives \"approximately $1.8 to $3.0 billion\" for Unit 1. It got there by taking a generic DOE (2023, Pathways to Commercial Liftoff) first-of-a-kind range of $6,000–10,000/kWe and multiplying by 300 MWe. It's an input to the local-spending impact estimate (13% spent locally), not a project estimate. It is far below TVA's own IRP assumption from seven months earlier ($17,949/kW).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"THE KEY FINDING: TVA's real construction-cost estimate exists and sits with the NRC, but it is withheld. The public Enclosure 1 (General and Administrative Information) says the estimate is in Appendix 1A, covering plant cost with contingency, the substation and the first core. Appendix 1A's figure is blanked and \"withheld\" under 10 CFR 2.390(a)(4), the confidential-commercial-information exemption. The ER itself points to Enclosure 1 for the overnight cost.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"Financing: the ER's cost table names the method (interest during construction at a 7% weighted average cost of capital, about 2% a year escalation) but gives no dollar figure for financing. TVA plans to fund Unit 1 through TVA debt, tax credits and grants, or third-party finance.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"The NRC's Final Supplemental EIS (April 2026) relays the ER range as \"TVA's current cost estimate\" and prints \"$1.8–3.0 billion overnight capital cost ($6,000 to $10,000 per kWe)\" in its internal-cost table. It explains the basis (the DOE report), so it doesn't present the range as a TVA project estimate. A public, government-printed Clinch River figure exists, but on air it must travel with \"a generic range TVA borrowed from a DOE report\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"The NRC staff reviewed TVA's financial qualification against the (withheld) Enclosure 1 in a separate safety evaluation, and the final safety evaluation report states no figure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"A27/E10 \"TVA estimated the first reactor at about $5.4 billion\": CORRECTED. No TVA document read prints $5.4 billion. The three IRP editions have 0 hits for \"5.4 billion\" or \"$5.4\", and the draft has 3 hits for \"17,949\", all in its tables. $5.4B is $17,949/kW x 300 MW (our arithmetic: $5.38B). Search summaries attribute that sum to a Knox News report (not opened). TVA's own table uses 285 MW, which gives about $5.1B. \"Around $3.7 billion\" for later units is likewise $12,471 x 300 MW. Right wording: \"TVA's draft plan assumed about $18,000 per kilowatt for a first small reactor, in 2024 dollars; for a 300-megawatt unit that works out to roughly $5 billion before interest and tax credits.\" Error source: secondary relay (press arithmetic restated by trade press as \"TVA previously estimated\").","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"A31: the advocacy blog's Colorado PUC relay (\"$17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars\", overnight, \"TVA's recent filings\") matches the draft 2025 IRP's Table 3-3 exactly in figure, dollar-year and basis. That makes the draft IRP its almost certain origin (inference by exact match; the PUC exhibit 24A-0442E Hearing Exhibit 1701 was not opened). The blog says \"for the BWRX-300\". TVA's table says a generic first-of-a-kind light-water SMR, though the 2026 IRP text links that option to the BWRX.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"A28/E9 still stand on TVA's latest annual SEC filing read: the board has approved only up to $350M to explore new nuclear. A build decision needs a further board approval and the NRC. The 10-K carries no Clinch River capital cost. (The 10-Q to 30 Jun 2026 also carries none; see A29.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"Board materials 2025–2026 (build decision, construction budget, financing): not read. tva.com board pages return a bot-check 403 and weren't bypassed. Two searches found no board vote on a build decision or a construction budget after the CP. Whether the board acted after the 10-Q (filed 4 Aug 2026), including at the August 2026 meeting that accepted the IRP, is UNRESOLVED. The latest filing read still has the DOE grant in the terms stage.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP: IPCC AR5 WG3 Annex III (2014), Table A.III.2, life-cycle emissions, gCO2eq/kWh, min / median / max: coal (pulverised) 740 / 820 / 910; gas combined cycle 410 / 490 / 650; hydropower 1.0 / 24 / 2200; nuclear 3.7 / 12 / 110; solar PV rooftop 26 / 41 / 60, utility 18 / 48 / 180; concentrated solar 8.8 / 27 / 63; wind onshore 7.0 / 11 / 56, offshore 8.0 / 12 / 35. The hydro maximum (2200) is reservoir methane at the worst sites; the median is the comparable figure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP: a US agency uses the IPCC medians as its yardstick: DOE's Final HALEU EIS (Oct 2024) gives coal 820, gas 490, hydro 24, solar 41 and nuclear 12 gCO2e/kWh, citing Schlömer et al. 2014 (= the IPCC annex, T2-1). Note DOE picked rooftop solar (41), not utility-scale (48).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP: NREL's LCA Harmonization for nuclear (Warner and Heath, J. Industrial Ecology 2012, DOE-funded; OSTI record): 274 references screened to 27 with 99 estimates, light-water reactors only. Published median 13, interquartile range 23, range 220 gCO2e/kWh; after harmonisation 12, 17 and 110. ⚠ \"110\" here is the RANGE (max minus min), not a maximum. Under falling uranium ore grades the median \"could be 9 to 110\" by 2050 depending on conditions.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP: NREL's 2021 update (fact sheet NREL/FS-6A50-80580, Sept 2021), Table 1 \"Median Published\" totals, gCO2e/kWh: nuclear 13 (light-water only; source Warner and Heath 2012), natural gas 486, coal 1001, wind 13, photovoltaic 43, hydropower 21. Nuclear's 13 is the PUBLISHED median; the harmonised median is 12 (T2-3). The odd spacing in the quotes (\"Fac tors\") is the PDF's own glyph spacing in layout extraction.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP: UNECE, \"Carbon Neutrality in the UNECE Region: Integrated Life-cycle Assessment of Electricity Sources\" (©2021, Geneva 2022; the brief's \"Life Cycle Assessment of Electricity Generation Options\"), its own model across 12 regions, gCO2e/kWh: coal 751–1095; gas combined cycle 403–513; nuclear 5.1–6.4 (5.5 global average); hydro 6–147; PV 8.0–83; CSP 27–122; wind onshore 7.8–16, offshore 12–23. A ranged model run, not a literature median: never put its 5.5 beside IPCC's 12 as if one source revised the other.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (equal rigour on the low figure): UNECE says its nuclear result sits in the LOWER range of the literature because of three assumptions (60-year plant life instead of 40, centrifuge-only enrichment, revised mining inputs with more in-situ leaching), and its nuclear inventory was updated \"with the support of the World Nuclear Association\" (the industry's trade body) and its expert network. Its nuclear fuel cycle is global average data, not regionalised.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (what drives nuclear's figure, UNECE): the front end, above all mining, dominates; core processes and back end contribute no more than 30% and 10% of impacts; diesel used on site is the main GHG cause in mining and milling. Enrichment assumed at 40 kWh per SWU on a weighted global electricity mix.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (what drives it, NREL): the primary energy mix behind mining and enrichment matters most; enrichment usually uses more primary energy than any other single step; enrichment method ALONE is a weak explanatory variable; mining and milling can be ~30% of the total (citing an earlier study).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (SMR LCAs, as UNECE reports them): UNECE built NO inventory for non-light-water SMRs \"due to a scarcity of data\"; for water-cooled SMRs it relied on two papers: a NuScale LCA at 4.6 gCO2e/kWh (ref. 108, a 2019 Clemson University master's thesis, not peer-reviewed) and Carless et al. 2016 (ref. 109, peer-reviewed). It concludes the SMR range \"coincides\" with large reactors (its own 5.6 under European conditions). UNECE's \"8.4\" for Carless is wrong: see T2-10.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP: the only PEER-REVIEWED SMR life-cycle study found: Carless, Griffin and Fischbeck, Energy 114 (2016) 84–99 (Carnegie Mellon), a prospective LCA with Monte Carlo. Westinghouse SMR mean 9.1 (90% CI 5.9–13.2) gCO2e/kWh; the large AP1000 8.4 (5.5–12.1); the AP1000 is 9% LOWER than the SMR; without the fuel cycle the two are \"effectively the same\"; an older Gen II plant (SNUPPS) 13.6. CORRECTED: UNECE (T2-9) and the Clemson thesis (T2-11) give \"8.4\" as the SMR's figure; 8.4 is the AP1000's. Right wording: \"the one peer-reviewed SMR study found its SMR about 9 g, slightly above a large reactor's 8 g.\" Error source: the source's own wording (UNECE, relaying the thesis). Only the abstract was read (RePEc); the Elsevier full text was not opened.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t2-10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP: the NuScale LCA UNECE relays is a 2019 Clemson University master's thesis (not peer-reviewed): 4.55 kg CO2-eq per MWh (= 4.55 g/kWh), with most of the climate impact in fuel processing (electricity and fossil fuel demand) and in construction (concrete). It modelled the earlier 12-module, 720 MWe NuScale plant (UNECE Table 12), not today's 77 MWe-module designs. Its \"slightly better than their larger counterparts\" rests partly on the misread 8.4 (T2-10).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP: a 2023 De La Salle University (Manila) master's thesis (not peer-reviewed; abstract only read) modelled a NuScale VOYGR plant of 924 MWe gross in the Philippines: 2.68 kg CO2-eq per MWhe (= 2.68 g/kWh); enrichment was the biggest global-warming step. Lower than the large-reactor figures, which the author himself calls lower than expected.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (BWRX-300): no complete LCA of the BWRX-300 was found. An Ontario Tech University conference paper (Canadian Nuclear Society annual conference, June 2024) \"initiates\" one for Darlington: construction only so far (concrete and steel, scaled from a large reactor); the fuel cycle and the rest are still to come. Absence finding from a limited search (7 WebSearches), not a proof that none exists.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (VENDOR claim): NuScale's marketing page charts \"NuScale SMR\" 4.55 against \"Conventional Nuclear\" 5.13 gCO2-eq/kWh, citing UNECE 2021, and says its SMR \"has even lower life cycle emissions than the conventional nuclear average.\" CORRECTED: the 4.55 is the unreviewed thesis (T2-11) that UNECE relays; UNECE's conventional global average is 5.5, not 5.13 (5.13 is not in UNECE's text; it may be a regional chart value: unverified); UNECE itself says the SMR range \"coincides\" with large reactors (T2-9); the one peer-reviewed study has the SMR 9% ABOVE a large reactor (T2-10). Right wording: \"on the studies that exist, an SMR's life-cycle carbon looks about the same as a big reactor's.\" Error source: the vendor's own wording.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t2-14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (HALEU, from the industry's trade body, World Nuclear Association): most SMR and advanced designs plan 10–20% HALEU; a listed cost is \"more energy required for enrichment\", a listed benefit higher burnup (more energy per kg of fuel); because HALEU cascades are fed with ~5% material, about 85% of the separative work is already done at the feed stage. No per-kWh life-cycle figure for any HALEU-fuelled reactor was found (and UNECE built none for non-light-water SMRs, T2-9).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-15"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (HALEU, DOE's bounding estimate): the Final HALEU EIS (Oct 2024) puts the GHG of producing and using up to 290 t of HALEU (mining to reactor operations, incl. truck transport) at 769,703 to 2,454,990 t CO2e, of which HALEU enrichment 10,481 to 179,019 t and mining and milling 695,317 to 1,473,763 t (the largest step); the fuel would make 385 to 569 million MWh. OUR ARITHMETIC: about 1.4 to 6.4 gCO2e/kWh at the two extremes, with enrichment roughly 1% to 7% of the total. NOT a life-cycle assessment: an EIS activity inventory whose scope for building the reactors' materials is not stated in the passages read. Off air as a per-kWh figure; usable only as \"DOE's own estimate puts enrichment far behind mining.\" DOE says the HALEU power would emit 94% to over 99% less than the existing grid mixes it replaces.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-16"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (critique 1, the high-figure study): the best-known high nuclear figures (Storm van Leeuwen 2007) used the \"AEI\" (energy-input) method; NREL's harmonisation EXCLUDED all AEI studies as producing \"inaccurately high outlier estimates\". Equal rigour: NREL did not drop the critics' central point. It re-ran a falling-ore-grade scenario \"similar to\" Storm van Leeuwen's without the AEI method, which gives the 9-to-110 g median by 2050 in T2-3. The exclusion is the harmonisers' methodological judgement, citing other reviewers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-17"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (critique 2, the high average): Sovacool's 2008 review (Energy Policy) gives a MEAN of 66 gCO2e/kWh, range 1 to 288, from 19 of 103 studies. NREL says the gap with its median is \"almost exclusively\" its exclusion of AEI studies plus median versus mean. Secondary-verified: Sovacool's own paper was not opened (RePEc 404, figshare refused); read as relayed by NREL (here) and by Jacobson (who gives the range as 1.4 to 288).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-18"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP (critique 3, delay): a Stanford professor and advocate of all-renewable systems, in a 2019 chapter for his own 2020 book (not a journal paper), puts nuclear's life cycle at 9–70 g, then ADDS 64–102 g of \"opportunity cost\" (the grid's emissions during 10–19 years of planning and building against 2–5 for wind or solar) plus heat, water-vapour and weapons-risk terms, for a total of 78–178 g. The total is a different measure from T2-1 to T2-5. By his own wording his life-cycle range is \"well within\" the IPCC's. On SMRs he writes that their emissions and time lag \"are still not known\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-19"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: the Navy's record, in the program's own 2025 report (statistics as of December 2024): more than 7,600 reactor-years, more than 177 million miles, 97 reactors in operation now, 230 nuclear ships built, 457 cores taken critical (refuellings included). The rebuttal's \"60 years\" undersells it: a former program director quoted in the report speaks of \"75 years\". \"97 reactors\" is more than the 77 active ships. A likely reason is that carriers carry two reactors and the program runs land-based and moored training reactors, but the split isn't in the passages read (unverified).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: the safety record AS THE PROGRAM STATES IT: no reactor accident and no release of radioactivity harmful to health or the environment in its whole history. This is the program's own claim about itself, and the program is its own overseer (T3-3). Attribute it (\"the Navy says\").","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: who oversees it. Under statute (50 U.S.C. §§ 2406, 2511, codifying Executive Order 12344), Naval Reactors holds total responsibility for design, construction, operation, safety and training, under central technical control. The commercial safety regulator (the NRC) licenses commercial plants, so a commercial SMR answers to a different regulator and a different licensing process. That point is our inference, which the text states only by contrast (\"in accordance with its Nuclear Regulatory Commission license\" for commercial reactors).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: fuel. Naval reactors burn highly enriched uranium at 93% U-235. The Navy's own 2014 report to Congress studied a 5%-enriched submarine core (roughly commercial-reactor level) and called it infeasible and cost prohibitive. Weapons-grade-level fuel can't go into a commercial fleet. Note that most SMRs on the bench use under-5% LEU (BWRX-300, NuScale), and some use HALEU below 20% (Natrium, X-energy): see hand pass D.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: why HEU, in the Navy's words: life-of-ship cores. A Virginia-class submarine core runs the 33-year ship life without refuelling; on 20% LEU it would need three refuellings, about $1.0 billion more per ship over its life. The cost the Navy weighs is ship-level life-cycle cost and fleet availability, not cost per megawatt-hour.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: why naval designs don't carry over, in the program's own words. Naval reactors must meet \"unique design criteria\": ruggedness, endurance, manoeuvrability and compactness \"far greater than land-based reactors\", fuel built for shock loads more than 10 times the seismic loads assumed on land, rapid power changes, and quiet running. A grid plant needs none of these and is judged on cost per unit of electricity.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: the supply chain. Naval components are built to standards the program calls far more stringent than civilian ones (battle shock, noise, crew proximity, rapid power changes). Its heavy components take 4-8 years of precision machining, welding, heat treatment and testing. Two private shipyards build every nuclear ship. A slow, bespoke, military-spec chain is not a template for cheap civilian units.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: is cost a design constraint? Yes, but not the grid's kind. The Navy writes that a plant \"must be compact\" to be cost-effective, and that the HEU design gives \"very long core lifetimes for maximum affordability and ship readiness\". The cost being minimised is the warship's and the fleet's, where compactness and endurance come first.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: what nuclear power costs the Navy. GAO's 1998 study (GAO/NSIAD-98-1) put 50-year life-cycle costs at $14.1 billion for a conventional carrier against $22.2 billion for a nuclear carrier, in fiscal-1997 dollars. The Defense Department agreed there is a nuclear life-cycle premium but disputed GAO's size of it. Caveats: 28 years old; whole-carrier life-cycle cost, not a reactor price; the conventional baseline was older ships. A per-reactor unit cost was NOT found in any source opened: off air.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1, what the analogy gets RIGHT (1): the Navy's technology already IS commercial nuclear's ancestry. The program says its technology is the basis of civilian nuclear power worldwide: uranium-dioxide fuel, large PWR components, safety and containment standards. It built Shippingport (1957), whose PWR design became the prototype for most commercial stations. So \"the Navy knows how\" is true, and the US grid already runs on that know-how at large scale.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1, what the analogy gets RIGHT (2): the program's own account of its success is the SMR pitch. A small number of standard designs; one headquarters setting standards for all work; long-standing repeat suppliers; small, inherently stable PWRs; and a deep training pipeline (more than 127,000 enlisted personnel trained or in training). Standardisation, repetition and trained crews are exactly what SMR backers say will bring costs down. The analogy holds on HOW to build, not on what it costs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: Vogtle 3 and 4's total cost. An MIT CANES report (ANP-201, July 2024, sole academic author) puts the realised total at about $35 billion \"in 2023 USD\". Its footnote shows this is DERIVED: Georgia Power's January 2024 total cost divided by its 45.7% share, assuming the co-owners' financing matched Georgia Power's. This agrees with INL's \"~$35B (Mixed USD)\" (A19). No filed all-owner total was opened. Say \"about $35 billion, by independent estimates, financing included\", never a filed figure.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: who paid at Vogtle (Georgia Power's share only). The Georgia PSC's Order Adopting Stipulation (Docket 29849, decided 19 Dec 2023) put $7.562 billion of construction and capital cost into rate base. That is $2.6 billion less than Georgia Power's $10.188 billion forecast at completion, so shareholders absorbed that gap. The original 2009 certification was $6.447 billion. Financing (AFUDC) on capital above $4.418 billion, up to $7.562 billion, also goes into rate base, and financing collected through the NCCR tariff came on top. So customers pay more than $7.562 billion all in. The co-owners' customers (about 54% of the plant) are outside this order.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: the bill. Georgia Power's own one-pager (August 2023, before the vote) estimated an average retail rate rise of about 5%, and $8.95 a month for a typical home using 1,000 kWh, with the NCCR tariff removed. This is the company's own estimate: attribute it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: V.C. Summer's cancellation. The same AP1000 design, contracted in 2008. SCE&G (55% owner) acted for itself and for Santee Cooper, the state-owned utility. Westinghouse and its construction affiliate went bankrupt in March 2017, Santee Cooper suspended work, and SCANA stopped construction in July 2017. The record of the 2007-09 wave is in B38.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-15"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: who paid at V.C. Summer (SCE&G customers), as Dominion's FY2018 10-K relays the South Carolina PSC's Dec 2018 SCANA Merger Approval Order: $2.4 billion excluded from rates (borne by the company); a $1.0 billion refund of amounts already collected from SCE&G customers, credited over about 11 years; and a remaining $2.8 billion regulatory asset collected from customers over 20 years with a return. In short, customers pay for two reactors that were never finished, well into the 2030s. The PSC order itself and Santee Cooper's customers' share were not opened (off air).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-16"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: the fraud. The US Attorney (D.S.C.) says SCANA's former CEO was sentenced to two years for defrauding ratepayers. Executives withheld schedule problems from regulators to keep rate increases and up to $2.2 billion in tax credits. Cooperation agreements with Dominion and Westinghouse provide over $4 billion in ratepayer relief. Name the role, not the person (a sentencing, not an official act).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-17"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: the administration's own large-reactor plan. Executive Order 14302 (May 2025) directs DOE to help reach 5 GW of uprates and \"10 new large reactors with complete designs under construction by 2030\". This answers the rebuttal: government policy already backs big reactors alongside SMRs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-18"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: the Westinghouse partnership (28 Oct 2025), in the companies' joint release filed by Cameco with the SEC: \"at least $80 billion\" of new Westinghouse reactors to be built in the US, with profit-sharing \"once certain thresholds are met\". The release names no site, no unit count and no price per unit, and says definitive agreements are still to be entered into. A framework, not an order book.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-19"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: the money, 23 June 2026 (this closes E12's unopened \"$17.5B AP1000 supply-chain\" item). DOE's Office of Energy Dominance Financing made a CONDITIONAL commitment of $17.5 billion for long-lead items covering five projects of two AP1000s each (10 reactors). Westinghouse and each partner must put up $500 million in equity apiece first. Westinghouse has letters of intent with seven potential partners; no site is named. Technical, legal, environmental and financial conditions must be met before any loan is funded. Not a loan yet, and not one reactor ordered.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-20"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: large vs small, from MIT (ANP-201, 2024 USD, a single-author academic projection). Next two AP1000s at Vogtle: about $8,300/kW overnight including owner's cost, 80-month build. Even after faster learning, the SMR's NOAK cost stays about 45% above the AP1000's. MIT expects new grid demand to be met \"by large reactors than SMRs, if sufficient support is provided\". For SMRs, it finds phased SMR deployment \"provides significant reduction in financed capital\" and risk, about $6 billion up front versus $20-30 billion for a pair of AP1000s. That is the small side's real argument: the size of the bet, not the price per kW.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-21"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2: DOE's own framing, cross-referenced from A20 (a draft Liftoff deck hosted by NARUC, watermarked not final): large reactors cheaper per kW, SMRs a smaller overall project. INL's Vogtle review (A19) adds that Unit 4 was \"~20% cheaper\" by DOE's account but calls the public data inconclusive. Same direction as MIT, but not independent: MIT's author is a common source in this literature, and DOE draws on it. That is our observation; the shared authorship isn't shown in the passages read here.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-22"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: Olkiluoto 3 (Finland), the vendor's cost. The French audit court (Cour des comptes, January 2025): a turnkey order to an Areva-Siemens consortium; regular output began April 2023, 13 years behind the original schedule; Areva's cost at completion rose from €1.6bn (2003) to €8.9bn, and all builders' cost incl. Siemens from €2.3bn to €9.5bn (current euros, financing excluded). This is the VENDOR's cost of building it, not the owner's price. Fetched from https://www.vie-publique.fr/files/rapport/pdf/296839.pdf","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: Olkiluoto 3, who carried it (vendor side). Under the fixed-price turnkey contract the overrun fell mainly on Areva, then majority state-owned: the court says the project's troubles helped break up the old Areva group; Areva paid €450m to the owner (TVO) plus €400m in delay penalties, and paid EDF €563m in 2021, financed by selling 9.4% of Orano to the French State. So French taxpayers, through a state company, carried much of a Finnish plant's overrun. (Areva's state ownership is general knowledge, not in the passages read: say \"France's state-backed vendor\" only if checked, or \"the French vendor\".)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: Olkiluoto 3, the owner's side. TVO's own stock-exchange release (11 March 2018): the arbitration over delay costs settled for €450m paid to TVO; TVO then put its own total investment at about €5.5bn. TVO is owned by Finnish industrial and energy companies and sells its output to them. So the owner's buyers also paid well above the contract, but the fixed price capped their exposure far below the vendor's (T4-1). The €3.2bn original contract price was not opened (snippet only). Fetched from https://mb.cision.com/Main/1401/2469737/803819.pdf","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: Flamanville 3 (France). Start-up 12 years late. EDF's construction-cost target was €3.2-3.3bn in May 2006 (EDF's table restates it in 2015 euros), for grid connection mid-2012; by December 2022 it was €13.2bn (2015 euros), loading in early 2024. The court puts the full cost, financing included, at €20.4bn (2015) = €23.7bn (2023 euros). Name the measure: construction-only €3.3bn to €13.2bn (about four times); all-in €23.7bn.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: who carries the French overruns. EDF builds Flamanville for itself and owns Hinkley's majority; the court says EPR overruns put the risk on \"the shareholder, that is, the State\". So the French case lands on the state's balance sheet (taxpayers), not on a ratepayer surcharge as in Georgia (T3-13).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: Hinkley Point C (UK), the first official cost. The UK National Audit Office (June 2017): the developer expects about £18bn to build it (in 2016 prices), financed in full by its two investors (EDF 66.5%, CGN 33.5%), generating from 2025. The court of audit also says first power was \"initially planned in 2025\". Fetched from https://www.nao.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2017/06/Hinkley-Point-C.pdf","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: Hinkley Point C, the latest official cost (EDF's 2025 annual results, 20 Feb 2026): completion cost £35bn in 2015 sterling, Unit 1 production moved to 2030; each further 12-month delay adds about £1bn (2015 sterling). That is roughly double the 2017 figure (£18bn in 2016 prices: different base years, so \"about double\", never an exact ratio) and about five years late for Unit 1. The same release: Flamanville 3 reached 100% power. Read at the archived copy https://web.archive.org/web/2026/https://www.edf.fr/sites/groupe/files/2026-02/annual-results-2025-pr-en-2026-02-20.pdf (edf.fr refused the download).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: Hinkley in today's money and the slipping dates (the court, January 2025). January 2024's £31-34bn (2015) was up about 30% on May 2022's £25-26.1bn; at the top of the range that is £46.5bn nominal. EDF's own table: Unit 1 target December 2025 (Sept 2019), June 2026 (Jan 2021), June 2027 (May 2022), 2030 (Jan 2024); Unit 2 is assumed 12 months after Unit 1. The 2026 £35bn has no nominal conversion in a primary read (press figures of £48-49bn are off air).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: Hinkley, who pays (the NAO). The contract for difference pays the developer £92.50/MWh (2012 prices) for 35 years; when the market price is lower, bill payers pay the top-up. Construction risk sits with the developer: \"the first time\" this was done for nuclear. The NAO's own warning: past experience shows these risks \"could shift back\" to taxpayers or consumers. In 2016 the expected top-ups had a present value of £30bn. So: bill payers don't pay the overrun, but they do pay a fixed price that was set high precisely because the developer took that risk.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: how the overrun bites EDF, not the bill (the court). The strike price is £92.50 (2012), inflation-linked, cut to £89.50 if Sizewell C went ahead (it did: EDF's 2025 release booked a €2.5bn impairment for that cut). The 35-year term shrinks if the plant is late, and the government may end the contract if Unit 1 is very late (deadline moved from 1 Nov 2033 to 1 Nov 2036). EDF wrote the asset down 43% at end-2023.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: Barakah (UAE, Korea's APR1400), the counter-record, as relayed by the World Nuclear Association (an industry body, secondary). Four units; a contract of about $20.4bn, largely fixed-price; Barakah One took over $24.4bn of project finance (a FINANCING package, not an outturn cost). Commercial operation was due 2017-2020 and came April 2021, March 2022, February 2023 and September 2024: each unit about four years late. Unit 1's construction finished March 2018; the main delay was operational readiness: the UAE regulator's 400-plus adverse findings postponed start-up by at least 18 months. No owner-published final cost was found: the record is thin. Fetched from https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/country-profiles/countries-t-z/united-arab-emirates","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t4-11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: Barakah's original schedule in a 2013 presentation at an OECD-NEA workshop (author from the Korean side): ENEC's announced schedule had Unit 1's first concrete July 2012 and commercial operation by May 2017. Confirms the \"about four years late\" for Unit 1 (April 2021, T4-11). Fetched from https://www.oecd-nea.org/ndd/workshops/wpne/presentations/docs/42KIM%20Barakah%20presentation.pdf","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: the Korean cost record behind Barakah. Lovering et al. (2016, 349 reactors) found South Korea alone with a continuous cost decline (cross-ref A17; a single utility; disputed by Koomey et al., A18). This is the best evidence that big-reactor overruns are not universal, and it is about Korea building repeatedly at home, not about Western builders.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(a): the record on nuclear learning (cross-ref A15). Rubin et al.'s review: four studies of nuclear learning rates range from negative to 6%; solar PV's median is 23%. So \"factories make it cheaper\" has not shown up in nuclear's history; it has in solar's.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(a): Gold's best evidence (MIT ANP-201, 2024, single author, a projection; cross-ref T3-21). A bottom-up learning rate of 8-15% is found for light-water reactors under a consistent regulator and a standardised design; MIT then ASSUMES 15% for a \"fully modularized\" BWRX-300 series. Even so its SMR series stays about 45% above an AP1000 series (T3-21). The learning rate is an assumption about the future, as is INL's 9.5% (A24).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-15"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(a): the BWRX-300's \"factory\" in practice. GE Hitachi gave BWXT the contract to make the reactor pressure vessel (the largest component) for the FIRST unit at Darlington, at BWXT's existing Cambridge, Ontario, nuclear shop: a one-unit order to an established heavy-component maker, not a series factory. The January 2025 release said commercial operation \"by the end of 2029\"; OPG's March 2026 report says grid connection by the end of 2030 (A2). Different milestone words, so not quoted as a slip. Fetched from https://www.gevernova.com/news/press-releases/ge-hitachi-awards-contract-bwrx-300-reactor-pressure-vessel-bwxt","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-16"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(a): what \"modular\" means at Darlington (ANS Nuclear Newswire, June 2026, existing file from pass B). The basemat's steel modules came from several Ontario shops for assembly on site; the reactor building alone will hold about 1,200 steel composite modules. Modular construction is real, but it is shop fabrication plus site assembly, not a reactor rolling off an assembly line.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-17"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(a): NuScale's factory claim vs its factory (cross-ref B30). The 10-K CLAIMS each module \"can be entirely fabricated in a factory\" (the company's belief), and reports forgings for 12 upper vessels progressing at Doosan, ordered \"in advance of customer orders\". The company has no firm customer order (the Utah project ended, A11).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-18"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(a): the one SMR-dedicated factory in the record is a FUEL plant. X-energy's TRISO-X got an NRC licence (Feb 2026) for two fuel plants, TX-1 and TX-2, for 40 years; TX-1 is under construction at Oak Ridge, TX-2 is in design, and the NRC must still inspect before any HALEU arrives. X-energy's \"operations in 2028\" is a search snippet of its S-1 only (off air). Fetched from https://www.ans.org/news/2026-02-17/article-7762/nrc-grants-license-for-trisox-fuel-manufacturing-using-haleu/","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-19"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(b): the closest real \"fleet grounding\": France 2022, in EDF's own results (17 Feb 2023). French nuclear output 279 TWh, 81.7 TWh below 2021, mainly from inspections and repairs for stress corrosion; the 16 most sensitive reactors were all of two recent series (N4 and P'4), 10 already treated; the output loss cost EDF an estimated €29.1bn of EBITDA because it had to buy power at high prices; the government's cap on consumer prices cost EDF another €8.2bn. Only 30 of 56 reactors were running on 1 Nov 2022 (not all from corrosion). Fetched from https://echanges.dila.gouv.fr/OPENDATA/AMF/MKW/2023/02/FCMKW11318720230217.pdf (EDF's release as filed with the French market authority).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-20"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(b): what it did to the grid and prices (RTE, France's grid operator, 16 Feb 2023). Nuclear availability 54% against a 2015-19 average of 73%; output lowest since 1988, with stress-corrosion checks among the causes; France a net IMPORTER for the first time since 1980 (16.5 TWh); French spot prices averaged €612/MWh in the week of 22 August; forward prices for winter 2022-23 carried a France-specific risk premium RTE calls out of proportion to the real risk; the price rise passes into tariffs during 2023. Caveat: 2022 was also Europe's gas crisis (T4-22). Fetched from https://assets.rte-france.com/prod/public/2023-02/Communique-presse-bilan-electrique-20220.pdf","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-21"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(b): the bill for the country (Banque de France note, 2023, an estimate by its economists). Nuclear unavailability added about €16bn to France's 2022 energy bill; the corrosion was found in autumn 2021, EDF stopped 10 reactors, about 10 GW on average, 82 TWh lost. The same note attributes the rise in electricity prices to gas prices. So: the flaw cost France's trade balance billions, but the price spike was mainly gas; don't say the cracks caused Europe's 2022 price crisis. Fetched from https://www.banque-france.fr/system/files/2023-11/Soldeenergetique%20en%202022%20la%20crisedelaproductionelectronucleairesurvenueaupiremoment.pdf","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-22"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(b): an FAA-style grounding in US nuclear regulation, the clearest case found: on 13 March 1979 the NRC ordered five power reactors to shut down within 48 hours pending reanalysis, because one architect-engineer's piping-stress computer program had summed earthquake loads wrongly. A shared design-analysis error, not a shared hardware flaw; an analogy for what a common SMR design flaw could do, not an SMR event. Read at the archived copy https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.nrc.gov/reading-rm/doc-collections/gen-comm/info-notices/1979/in79006.html (the nrc.gov URL returned 404).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-23"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2(b): the standing tool. NRC Bulletins ask licensees to act on significant safety issues of \"great urgency\" and require a written answer: the mechanism by which one finding reaches every plant of a design at once. Fetched from https://www.nrc.gov/reading-rm/doc-collections/gen-comm/bulletins/index.html","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-24"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3(a): the calm, dark winter week, from the reliability regulator (NERC 2025-26 Winter Reliability Assessment, Nov 2025). All areas are adequate for a normal winter peak; wide, prolonged cold could cause shortfalls. Winter peaks come before sunrise and after sunset, when solar is gone, leaving wind and dispatchable plants. Batteries help, but keeping them charged gets harder through a \"severe multi-day storm like Winter Storm Uri\", with little time to recharge; some extreme events will drain them when needed. This SUPPORTS the viral claim's core: today's batteries are not a multi-day reserve. Fetched from https://prod.nerc.com/globalassets/our-work/assessments/nercwra2025.pdf","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-25"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3(a): the same NERC report on the other half. The named winter threats are forced outages of THERMAL plants and gas fuel supply in long cold snaps, plus lower intermittent output. Since last winter batteries added 11,121 MW of on-peak capacity, while wind's on-peak value fell 14,238 MW, mostly through revised accounting in ERCOT and MISO (footnote 6), not lost turbines.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-26"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3(a): Winter Storm Uri, Feb 2021 (FERC-NERC final report). 23,418 MW of manual firm load shed, the largest controlled firm load shed in US history; more than 4.5 million people in Texas lost power. Of the 1,045 generating units with outages: 58% gas-fired, 27% wind, 6% coal, 2% solar, under 1% nuclear (a COUNT of units, not MW). Freezing (44.2%) and fuel issues (31.4%) caused 75% of outages. Wind turbines were the largest share of units that froze. Verdict on the viral framings: Uri was not \"renewables failed\" (gas was most of the failing units, and fuel supply was a top cause) and not \"nuclear never fails\" (a nuclear unit is among the outages). Fetched from https://nerc.com/pa/rrm/ea/Documents/February2021ColdWeatherReport.pdf","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-27"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3(a): Winter Storm Elliott, Dec 2022 (FERC-NERC-Regional Entity report, Oct 2023). 90,500 MW of coincident unplanned outages at the worst point. Gas-fired units were 47% of the incremental unplanned loss by number of outages and 63% by MW (the numerals sit in a tab-separated line of the extraction, so they are not quoted; the line's end is). Gas fuel issues were 20% of all causes (83% of fuel-issue outages). Freezing caused 53% of wind outages by MW, and 75% of nuclear outages by MW, though the report notes freezing is historically rare in nuclear units. Grid operators shed over 5,400 MW of firm load (same tab-separated problem: our reading of p. 4 of the report). Gas was nearly 42% of the event area's generation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-28"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3(a): how long batteries last (EIA, 2022). Units built through 2020 averaged about 3.0 hours at full power; 4-8 hour batteries cycle once a day, shifting midday solar to the evening. Dated: no fleet-wide average newer than 2020 was found in an EIA primary. Hours, not days, either way: a calm winter week is about 168 hours (our arithmetic). Fetched from https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=51798","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t4-29"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3(a): how much battery there now is (EIA, 2026): 43.6 GW at end-2025 and nearly 52 GW of nameplate by mid-2026. These are POWER figures (GW), not stored energy; the current average duration was not found, so the fleet's total hours of storage are UNRESOLVED. Fetched from https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=67925","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t4-30"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3(b): \"local reactors avoid long transmission lines\", in DOE's 2024 coal-to-nuclear guide (existing file; cross-ref E20, E21). DOE says advanced nuclear \"does not need a lot of transmission buildout\", and a utility can reuse the coal plant's grid connection and switchyard. It also warns that a gap between the coal plant closing and the reactor starting can lose transmission rights. The viral claim holds for a reactor that takes over an existing plant's connection; it is not a property of nuclear in general.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-31"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3(b): the limit (INL for DOE, 2022 coal-to-nuclear report, the full report behind E20). Reusing a coal plant's electrical gear typically needs the new reactors to be the same size or smaller; a 500 MW coal plant could take 1-5 Xe-100 units and reuse most of it, but an AP1000 at a smaller coal site would need transmission upgrades. The report quotes another study: new lines can exceed $3M a mile and take years to approve and build. Fetched from https://fuelcycleoptions.inl.gov/SiteAssets/SitePages/Home/C2N2022Report.pdf","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-32"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3(b): the queue (Berkeley Lab, Queued Up 2025 edition, data to end-2024; cross-ref B35). The median project completed in 2024 waited 55 months from interconnection request to operation (that year's sample is dominated by ERCOT, which runs short). Nuclear is in the queue too: about 5.3 GW active in 2024, and 21% of nuclear capacity requested 2000-2019 was built, among the highest completion rates. An SMR on a fresh site would face the same queue; whether a coal-site replacement can skip it was NOT checked (UNRESOLVED on that point). The 2026 edition's \"61 months\" is a secondary summary only (off air). Read at the archived copy https://web.archive.org/web/2026/https://emp.lbl.gov/sites/default/files/2025-12/Queued%20Up%202025%20Edition%20-%2012.15.2025.pdf (bot check on the lab's site).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t4-33"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: the international definition. The IAEA defines SMRs as advanced reactors producing up to 300 MW(e) per module, deployable singly or as multi-module plants, 'designed to be built in factories'. Note the IAEA's own definition carries the factory claim as a design intent, not a fact about any built plant. Fetched from https://www.iaea.org/topics/small-modular-reactors via https://r.jina.ai/ (iaea.org 403).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: the US regulator's definition is THERMAL, not electric. The NRC's 2023 emergency-preparedness rule defines an SMR as a power reactor licensed up to 1,000 MWt per module; its fee rule's identical 1,000 MWt line is stated as the thermal equivalent of 300 MWe. 'Large LWRs' in that rule are above 1,000 MWt. Consequence: Natrium (840 MWt, 345 MW) is under the NRC's line but over the IAEA's 300 MWe line (T5-16, T5-17).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: DOE's Office of Nuclear Energy gives NO fixed size line on its SMR page: 'tens of megawatts up to hundreds of megawatts', with light water or non-light-water coolants (gas, liquid metal, molten salt). So '300 MW' is the IAEA's line and the NRC fee rule's equivalence, not a DOE definition. Fetched from https://www.energy.gov/ne/advanced-small-modular-reactors-smrs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1: today's US reactors. 94 operating reactors (March 2026); the newest, Vogtle 4, is 1,114 MW nameplate; the SMALLEST operating US reactors (Prairie Island's two units) are about 593 MW nameplate each, so every reactor running in the US today is roughly twice the size of the IAEA's SMR ceiling or more. Fetched from https://www.eia.gov/tools/faqs/faq.php?id=207&t=3.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q1 comparator: one Vogtle unit (1,114 MW, EIA) is about 3.7 BWRX-300s (300 MWe) — my arithmetic, nameplate against nominal net, so say 'nearly four'. The US fleet averages roughly 1,000 MW a reactor (EIA's 'about 97 GW' in May 2024 over 94 reactors in March 2026 — my arithmetic across two dates, hence APPROXIMATE). Fetched from https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=61963 (dated May 1, 2024).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t5-5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2a: what the peg reactor IS, in the regulator's words: a small modular boiling LIGHT-WATER reactor, core cooled by natural circulation, 870 MWt / 300 MWe. Ordinary water as coolant, the boiling-water family GE has built since the 1950s (T5-22); the novelty is size and simplification, not a new coolant or fuel.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2a: its parentage, per the applicant as the NRC relays it: the 'next evolution' of the ESBWR, which holds an NRC design certification, and the tenth generation of GE's BWRs. The applicant intends the smaller, simpler plant to cut staff, maintenance and security needs (an intent, not a finding). Vendor name trap: it is now GE Vernova Hitachi Nuclear Energy (GVH), not 'GE-Hitachi'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2a: no recirculation pumps. The vendor: the SBWR/ESBWR used a taller vessel to get natural recirculation 'without the use of pumps'; the BWRX-300 keeps natural circulation without needing a shorter core, so it can use the fleet's existing fuel bundles. Its own table: ABWR ~1,350 MWe and ESBWR ~1,600 MWe gross against the BWRX-300's ~316 MWe gross — the ESBWR it descends from is a gigawatt-class plant. Vendor's document (GE Vernova Hitachi, BWRX-300 General Description, Rev. J, June 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2a: ordinary fuel, per the regulator: GNF2 assemblies with no design differences from the GNF2 fuel used in existing BWRs. (Enrichment in T5-10; the HALEU question doesn't touch this reactor, see D1-D3.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2a: enrichment 3.81% average, 4.95% maximum — low-enriched uranium under 5%, the vendor's table.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2a/Q3: 'passive' safety, in the NRC staff's own evaluation: the safety systems need no operator action or external power 'for at least 72 hours' after a design-basis accident; the staff finds the ICS's 7-day decay-heat function met. But the staff also records that for small pipe breaks the design takes credit for a diesel-powered pump adding water AFTER 72 hours. So the regulator's line is 'three days hands-off', not 'indefinitely'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2a: the vendor's 'days without power' claim: with two of three isolation-condenser trains, decay heat removal lasts seven days without operator action, and the pools can be topped up from a fire truck. This is the VENDOR's claim; the NRC's own words are T5-11 (72 hours hands-off; 7-day pool capacity).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: the independent reviewers (ACRS) on 'new': they call it an innovative natural-circulation BWR, but say BWR technology is well established; the novel part is natural circulation at this scale, where little operating data exists from the few historical natural-circulation BWRs ('a validation data gap'). Open items for the operating licence: natural-circulation stability, ICS performance, and the design's 'practical elimination' claims for beyond-design-basis events.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: 'can't melt down' / 'walk-away safe' is WRONG for the BWRX-300. Neither phrase appears in the vendor's description, the NRC's evaluation or the ACRS letter (grep, 2 Nov 2026); the evaluation's only 'meltdown' hits are the hypothetical siting accident in T5-15. The vendor's own design includes a CORE CATCHER for molten core (corium) after a reactor-vessel failure, i.e. it plans for a meltdown it calls a severe accident. Right wording: 'designed to cool itself for days without power or operators, with backstops if it doesn't.' Error origin: popular shorthand, not any primary in this record.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t5-14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: and the NRC has not yet judged severe accidents: core-damage scenarios go into the final safety report at the operating-licence stage, not the construction permit. So no NRC finding on 'meltdown-proof' exists yet for this design. And for siting, the regulations make the applicant ASSUME a hypothetical 'substantial meltdown of the core' and show the public is still protected — a required conservative test, not a prediction; never quote it as 'the NRC expects a meltdown'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-15"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2b: Natrium (Kemmerer, Wyoming) is a genuinely different machine: a pool-type SODIUM-cooled fast reactor, 840 MWt, metallic HALEU fuel, with molten-salt tanks storing heat between reactor and turbine (NRC's project page). Its fuel bottleneck is D11; its build record is B25-B26.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-16"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2b: Natrium's size: 345 MW, boostable to 500 MW from storage (developer's release). Above the IAEA's 300 MW(e) SMR line, though under the NRC's 1,000 MWt line (T5-2). Don't call Natrium an SMR without 'by the US regulator's measure'; the developer calls it an 'advanced reactor'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-17"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2b: X-energy's Xe-100 (the peg's AEP/Amazon Indiana project): 80 MWe, HELIUM-cooled high-temperature gas reactor, TRISO pebble fuel (15.5% HALEU per D5). Its 'without melting' line is the developer quoting DOE about the FUEL, not a regulator's finding about the plant; attribute it if used.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-18"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2b: the regulator's description of the Xe-100 at Long Mott (Dow, Texas): four 'small modular' helium-gas-cooled high-temperature reactors with solid TRISO fuel. So the NRC itself uses 'small modular' for this design.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-19"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q2b: Kairos Hermes (Oak Ridge): a 35 MWt TEST reactor (not a power plant), molten FLUORIDE-SALT-cooled, TRISO pebbles with HALEU; its containment relies principally on the fuel particles themselves (NRC safety evaluation). See D6. Never present Hermes as an SMR power plant.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-20"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: small reactors are not new. Shippingport (1957), the first full-scale civilian nuclear plant in the US, ran at 60,000 kilowatts (60 MW) — a fifth of a BWRX-300. DOE's Naval Nuclear Laboratory page. Navy ancestry is T3-10. Fetched from https://navalnuclearlab.energy.gov/bettis-atomic-power-laboratory.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-21"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: nor are small or natural-circulation BWRs. The vendor's own history: GE's first BWR, Vallecitos (1957), was 5 MWe; Dresden 1 (180 MWe) began commercial power in 1960; natural-circulation BWRs run from Vallecitos through Humboldt Bay and Dodewaard (58 MWe, 1969 to 1997) to the BWRX-300. Vendor's account.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-22"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: Big Rock Point (Michigan) — the NRC's 1995 notice confirms it went critical in 1962 and was licensed to run to 2000, but gives no size; 'about 67 MWe' is search-snippet only (the NRC's own page refused every route). Fetched from https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-1995-05-09/html/95-11365.htm. Use Shippingport instead, or say 'small' without a number.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t5-23"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: passive safety is not unique to SMRs. Vogtle 3/4's big AP1000s already have passive safety systems meant to shut down without operators or outside power (EIA). What the BWRX-300 adds is passive cooling at a smaller scale with fewer systems — not the idea itself.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-24"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: the smaller emergency zone. The NRC's 2023 rule (effective 18 Dec 2023) gives SMRs and other new technologies an OPTIONAL performance-based alternative with a scalable plume emergency planning zone, sized where projected public dose would exceed 1 rem over 96 hours; today's fleet uses about 10 miles; where the zone stops at the site boundary, the NRC does not mandate offsite radiological emergency planning.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-25"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: Clinch River's zone is NOT yet decided. Its 2019 early site permit approved two alternatives (zone at the site boundary, or about 2 miles), via exemptions; TVA chooses at a later stage. The section read describes the ESP's exemptions, not the 2023 rule's route. So never 'Clinch River needs no evacuation zone'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t5-26"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: 'modular = built in a factory' is the PROMISE (DOE, 2020: parts 'will be factory-made'; IAEA T5-1 'designed to be'). For the BWRX-300 the vendor's own document gives NO factory share and describes something else: steel-plate panels made in factories, assembled into modules AT SITE, lifted into a reactor building dug as a shaft on site; the reactor vessel itself is a 27.4 m, ~650-tonne piece needing barge or heavy-haul routes. Right wording: 'much of it prefabricated, but still built on site'. Error origin: DOE's and the IAEA's general framing applied to a specific design (and common press relay). DOE article fetched from https://www.energy.gov/ne/articles/4-key-benefits-advanced-small-modular-reactors (dated May 28, 2020).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t5-27"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"TOPUP-Q3: the DOE promise itself, for the record (its own article, 2020): 'factory-made and shipped', hence 'quicker to build and less expensive'. A claim, not a result; the build-speed and cost record is hand passes A and B.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t5-28"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"\"Clinch River's earliest completion date is about five years from now.\" Working: 2026-11-02 to 2031-10-01 is 4 years, 10 months and 29 days. The date is the permit's earliest completion date (C1), not an operation date; an operating licence is still needed (C2, C6).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w1"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"\"Of Meta's 'up to 6.6 GW', about 2.5 GW is power from plants already running and about 4 GW is new reactors that don't exist yet.\" Working (E4's parts): existing, more than 2.1 GW of purchases from two operating Vistra plants + 433 MW of uprates = about 2.5 GW; new, 690 MW (two Natrium units) + 2.1 GW (rights to up to six more Natrium units) + up to 1.2 GW (Oklo's campus) = 3.99 GW. The listed parts sum to about 6.5 GW; Meta's \"up to 6.6\" carries the \"more than\". Every new part is \"up to\", \"rights\" or \"poised to\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w2"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"\"In the nuclear agency's own table, a first SMR's power costs about one and a half times a gas plant's; one from a mature series would cost a bit less than the gas plant's.\" Working (A23, Table ES.2 medians, same study, 5% real discount rate, plant-level, dollar-year not stated in the sections read): near-term SMR $146/MWh ÷ CCGT $98 = 1.49; nth-of-a-kind SMR $84 < CCGT $98 and < solar PV + battery $94, > onshore wind $59. \"The tenth one\" on air is the show's shorthand for nth-of-a-kind; no source says ten.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w3"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"\"On EIA's figures a generic SMR costs about ten times a combined-cycle gas plant per kilowatt to build.\" Working (A33, overnight, 2023 dollars): $8,936/kW ÷ $868/kW = 10.3. Caveats that travel with it: a generic 480 MW SMR, not a BWRX-300; EIA's gas figure predates the turbine price rise Lazard prices at $2,400–2,600/kW in its high case (A22, A33). Say \"on EIA's 2023 figures\"; never set it beside Lazard's turbine prices.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w4"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"The peg hunt's draft description: \"Read the permit, though, and construction can't finish before October 2031.\" Overstated: the permit states an earliest completion date of October 1, 2031 (C1), which hand pass C reads as \"an earliest allowed completion date, not a forecast of operation\". The description now says \"its earliest completion date is October 1, 2031\", and adds that TVA still needs a licence to operate it (C2, C6).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-w5"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"The peg hunt's draft description: \"a utility, a reactor maker and Amazon agreed to study a 12-reactor plant in Indiana\"; and \"half of it is already settled\". The agreement is between AEP and X-energy; Amazon is \"a project development funder\" that intends to buy the power if the project reaches a final investment decision (E6, E7). The description now says \"a utility and a reactor maker agreed to study…, with Amazon paying for part of the development work\", and \"a lot of it\" for \"half of it\" (no row measures a half).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-w6"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"The peg hunt's Why now: \"the first small reactor they permitted cannot be finished before late 2031\". The regulator approved Kemmerer Unit 1 (TerraPower's 345 MW sodium-cooled Natrium) in March 2026, the first commercial non-light-water power-reactor permit (B24, B25, C8); Clinch River is the first plant the regulator calls a small modular reactor (C6). The brief now says so.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-w7"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"The peg hunt's Why now: \"the permits and rules are arriving faster than at any point in decades\". No row measures licensing pace across decades. Replaced with the measured review times: Clinch River about 14.6 months from docketing, Kemmerer about 21.6, Vogtle 3 and 4 (a combined construct-and-operate licence, 2008–12) about 44.4, none like-for-like (C3, C8, C9).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-w8"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"The NEA's sub-$100 round-the-clock figure (A23) is conditional on a mature series. Read in the saved report around A23's quotes: the sentence that follows the cost comparison makes the figure conditional on the nth-of-a-kind promise being realised, and the next says reaching that level needs standardised designs and repeat construction.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w9"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"The NEA chapter's \"near-term costs are roughly double those for NOAK large light-water reactors, and they are roughly 50% higher for SMRs\" (A23) against its own Table ES.2 medians. Working: large, $11,188 ÷ $5,502 per kWe = 2.03 (\"roughly double\" reproduces); $141 ÷ $81 per MWh = 1.74. SMR, $13,021 ÷ $7,092 per kWe = 1.84; $146 ÷ $84 per MWh = 1.74. The \"roughly 50%\" for SMRs does not reproduce from the table's medians (the chapter may use another statistic; not checked). Use the table's figures on screen, never the sentence's percentage.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-w10"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"\"A generic SMR costs about 3.6 times a gas plant per kW on current turbine prices, and about 14% more than a large reactor.\" Working: EIA AEO2025 generic SMR $8,936/kW (2023 dollars, overnight; A33) ÷ the midpoint of Lazard's $2,400–2,600/kW gas-turbine quotes ($2,500; A22) = 3.57; $8,936 ÷ EIA's 2×AP1000 $7,861/kW (A33) = 1.14. The first ratio mixes two sources and dollar-years: indicative only, never spoken as a precise figure.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-w11"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"\"TVA's long-term plan assumes about $17,000 a kilowatt for a first small reactor; for one unit that works out to roughly $5 billion.\" Working: final 2026 IRP, $17,263/kW (2026 dollars, overnight, before tax credits and before financing; T1-3, T1-4) × 285 MW (the IRP's net summer rating) = $4.92 billion; × 300 MW (the permit's nominal size) = $5.18 billion. Draft 2025 IRP, $17,949/kW (2024 dollars; T1-1) × 285 MW = $5.12 billion; × 300 MW = $5.38 billion, which is the press's \"$5.4 billion\" (T1-11). Every version rounds to \"roughly $5 billion\". The total is OUR arithmetic on a planning assumption for a generic first-of-a-kind option: never TVA's figure, never \"Clinch River will cost\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w12"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"\"TVA's first-unit assumption barely moved between the 2025 draft and the 2026 plan; its later-units assumption fell by about 30%.\" Working: first-of-a-kind $17,263 (2026 dollars) ÷ $17,949 (2024 dollars) = 0.962, about 4% lower in nominal terms; nth-of-a-kind $8,743 (2026 dollars) ÷ $12,471 (2024 dollars) = 0.701, about 30% lower (T1-1, T1-3). The two editions use different dollar-years, so in real terms the fall is larger than the nominal one if prices rose from 2024 to 2026; no row in the record measures that inflation, so no real-terms figure is stated. Both are re-estimates of planning inputs, not a price change on a project; the nth-of-a-kind fall is an assumption about future units, never evidence of learning.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w13"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"\"TVA's planners assume nearly twice the top of the range its permit paperwork borrowed.\" Working: the Environmental Report's generic DOE range is $6,000–10,000/kWe (DOE's 2023 figures; T1-6). $17,949 (2024 dollars) ÷ $10,000 = 1.79; $17,263 (2026 dollars) ÷ $10,000 = 1.73; against the bottom of the range, 2.9 to 3.0 times. In totals: the ER's $1.8–3.0 billion against roughly $4.9–5.4 billion on the IRP assumptions (W12). The dollar-years differ (2023, 2024, 2026), so \"nearly twice\" is indicative only; on screen, each figure carries its document and its year.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-w14"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"\"Over its life cycle, nuclear power emits about 12 grams of CO2 per kilowatt-hour: about the same as wind, roughly 40 times less than gas and 70 times less than coal.\" Working (IPCC AR5 medians, gCO2eq/kWh, one source; T2-1): gas combined cycle 490 ÷ nuclear 12 = 40.8; coal (pulverised) 820 ÷ 12 = 68.3; wind onshore 11 and offshore 12 against 12; utility solar 48 ÷ 12 = 4.0, rooftop 41 ÷ 12 = 3.4; hydropower median 24 ÷ 12 = 2.0. These are large light-water reactors: no SMR is in the IPCC's table.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w15"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"Carless et al.'s \"The GHG emissions of the AP1000 are 9% less than the W-SMR\" (T2-10) against the rounded means the same abstract gives. Working: (9.1 − 8.4) ÷ 9.1 = 7.7%; (9.1 − 8.4) ÷ 8.4 = 8.3%. The paper's 9% does not reproduce exactly from its rounded means (it may use unrounded values; abstract only, not checked). The 90% intervals overlap widely (5.9–13.2 against 5.5–12.1). Say \"about 9 grams against about 8: slightly higher\", never a precise percentage.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-w16"},{"s":"ep53-small-modular-reactors","n":53,"c":"\"Georgia Power's customers carry $7.562 billion of Vogtle's construction cost, $2.6 billion less than the company's forecast; Georgia Power owns under half the plant.\" Working: $10.188 billion − $7.562 billion = $2.626 billion, so the order's own \"$2.6 billion\" reproduces (T3-13); Georgia Power's share is 45.7% (T3-12), so the co-owners hold 54.3% (\"about 54%\"), outside this order. $7.562 billion is construction and capital cost only; financing is recovered on top (T3-13).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w17"}]);